add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Non-Dilated Left Ventricular Cardiomyopathy',
 'A patient investigated for ventricular ectopy has a normal-sized LV and LVEF 57%. CMR shows definite nonischemic myocardial scar. Coronary and loading-condition assessment does not explain the findings. Which ESC 2023 phenotype best describes this presentation?',
 'Non-dilated left ventricular cardiomyopathy',[
 ('Dilated cardiomyopathy','DCM requires LV dilatation with systolic dysfunction not explained solely by loading conditions or coronary disease. A normal-sized LV with preserved global EF does not meet that phenotype.'),
 ('Restrictive cardiomyopathy','Restrictive cardiomyopathy is characterized by restrictive ventricular physiology and the relevant structural-functional phenotype. Nonischemic scar alone does not establish restricted filling or that classification.'),
 ('Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy','HCM requires otherwise unexplained increased ventricular wall thickness. Scar can accompany HCM but cannot substitute for the absent hypertrophic phenotype in this case.')],
 [S('Classify the Observed Phenotype Before Naming the Cause','**Non-dilated LV cardiomyopathy can be present despite preserved global EF.** ESC 2023 includes unexplained nonischemic LV scar or fatty replacement without LV dilatation, with or without regional or global motion abnormalities.',
    'The category also includes isolated global LV hypokinesia without scar when abnormal loading conditions or coronary disease do not adequately explain it. It is broader than a requirement for both scar and reduced EF.',
    'Measure ventricular dimensions and function using appropriate body-size and sex context. A report of normal EF does not answer whether tissue disease or a regional functional abnormality exists.'),
  S('Move From Phenotype to Etiology and Risk','**A cardiomyopathy phenotype is the beginning of etiological assessment.** Review the pedigree, conduction and arrhythmic features, exposures, inflammatory presentations, extracardiac findings and CMR distribution.',
    'Inherited disease and prior inflammatory injury are possible explanations. Do not declare myocarditis, a particular gene or inevitable malignant arrhythmia from a nonischemic LGE pattern alone.',
    'Assess symptoms, ambulatory rhythm data and family history alongside scar and ventricular function. Some genetic substrates carry arrhythmic risk before marked EF reduction, but scar alone is not a universal ICD mandate. Treat documented HF or arrhythmia according to the established problem, arrange phenotype-directed genetic counseling where appropriate and reassess over time. A normal chamber size must not end investigation when reproducible tissue abnormalities are present. Conversely, an equivocal enhancement focus needs expert confirmation before assigning a lifelong inherited diagnosis.')],['CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Left Ventricular Hypertrabeculation',
 'CMR performed for an unrelated indication shows prominent LV trabeculation. Ventricular size and function are normal, there is no scar or thrombus, and history, ECG and family assessment are reassuring. Which interpretation best follows ESC 2023 classification?',
 'A morphological trait requiring interpretation within the overall clinical phenotype',[
 ('An imaging-defined cardiomyopathy based on the trabecular appearance alone','Older LV noncompaction labels can suggest a standalone disease from morphology. ESC 2023 instead treats hypertrabeculation as a trait; the reassuring function, tissue assessment and history do not supply an associated cardiomyopathy phenotype.'),
 ('A basis for preventive anticoagulation despite sinus rhythm and no thrombus','Thromboembolism is important when hypertrabeculation accompanies AF, thrombus or other relevant risk. The isolated reassuring trait does not itself establish that anticoagulation benefit exceeds bleeding risk.'),
 ('A basis for predictive family genotyping before establishing a familial cause','A defined cardiomyopathy or suggestive family phenotype can justify genetic evaluation. Isolated trabeculation does not identify a causal variant for meaningful predictive cascade testing in healthy relatives.')],
 [S('Separate a Morphological Descriptor From Disease','**ESC 2023 treats LV hypertrabeculation as a phenotypic trait rather than a cardiomyopathy category in its own right.** It can occur alone, with developmental abnormalities, or with a defined hypertrophic or dilated phenotype.',
    'The older term LV noncompaction can encourage an unsupported assumption that an imaging ratio establishes a single embryological disease. Different imaging definitions identify overlapping populations rather than one uniform prognosis.',
    'Physiological or acquired prominence can be seen in pregnancy and athletic adaptation. The assessment must still distinguish that context from dysfunction, scar, a familial phenotype or a syndromic disorder.'),
  S('Base Management on the Associated Clinical Abnormalities','**Assess ventricular function, arrhythmia, thrombus, embolic history and familial or extracardiac evidence.** Those findings determine investigation and treatment more directly than trabecular prominence alone.',
    'Anticoagulation is considered for established indications such as AF with the relevant risk context or documented ventricular thrombus. An isolated reassuring trabecular appearance is not a universal indication for anticoagulation or an ICD.',
    'When dysfunction or concerning arrhythmia accompanies the morphology, investigate the cardiomyopathy rather than dismissing it as a normal variant. Use expert image review if apical visualization or measurement is uncertain, and individualize follow-up according to the wider phenotype. A normal present evaluation is reassuring but should be revisited if syncope, ventricular dysfunction or a new familial diagnosis appears. Explain the finding in proportion to the evidence so an anatomical descriptor is not mistaken for proof of inevitable HF or sudden death.')],['CMP2023'])

R['CMRCONSENSUS']=('ACCF/ACR/AHA/NASCI/SCMR cardiovascular magnetic resonance consensus, 2010','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0b013e3181d44a8f')
add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Late Gadolinium Enhancement in Cardiomyopathy Evaluation',
 'CMR in a patient with unexplained LV dysfunction shows linear midwall septal late gadolinium enhancement, sparing the subendocardium. Which imaging mechanism directly accounts for the abnormal delayed postcontrast signal?',
 'Retention of extracellular contrast in tissue with an expanded distribution space',[
 ('Iron-related susceptibility causing a reduction in myocardial T2-star','Iron loading is assessed using susceptibility-sensitive T2-star measurements and can cause cardiomyopathy. It is not the mechanism of regional delayed gadolinium retention described here.'),
 ('Intracellular lipid storage producing a reduction in native myocardial T1','Low native T1 can provide a storage-disease clue, particularly in Fabry disease. That precontrast tissue property is distinct from the mechanism of delayed extracellular contrast enhancement.'),
 ('Increased tissue water identified directly by a myocardial T2 map','T2 mapping assesses edema and can complement LGE when investigating inflammation. It is a different measurement, and late enhancement alone does not directly establish active edema.')],
 [S('Contrast Distribution and Delayed Enhancement','**Conventional gadolinium agents distribute in the extracellular compartment, and expanded distribution space or altered washout produces late enhancement.** Fibrosis, necrotic injury and some infiltrative processes can create that contrast difference.',
    'LGE is distinct from native T1, T2 or T2-star mapping. The sequences answer related but different tissue questions, so their findings should not be treated as interchangeable.',
    'Acute tissue injury and chronic fibrosis can both enhance. The signal mechanism does not by itself identify the cause or establish that inflammation is currently active.'),
  S('Interpret Distribution Alongside Function and Edema','**LGE distribution provides etiological clues rather than an isolated molecular or histological diagnosis.** Subendocardial or transmural enhancement in a vascular territory supports ischemic injury; midwall or subepicardial patterns commonly support nonischemic injury.',
    'Septal midwall fibrosis is encountered in DCM, while inferolateral subepicardial injury can occur after myocarditis and in inherited substrates. Patterns overlap and do not eliminate the need to consider coronary disease when otherwise clinically plausible.',
    'T2-based edema assessment and T1-based injury findings contribute to myocarditis evaluation. Persistent enhancement can remain after acute inflammation resolves; LGE is not synonymous with ongoing viral replication or steroid-responsive disease.'),
  S('Use CMR to Refine Etiology and Prognosis','**Integrate the scan with the clinical course, ECG, biomarkers, coronary evaluation and family history.** CMR can identify occult scar despite a relatively preserved EF or an initially unrevealing echocardiogram.',
    'Scar can provide an arrhythmic substrate and add prognostic information, but the effect depends on disease, distribution, burden and the wider risk profile. Do not convert any positive LGE finding into an automatic device indication.',
    'Consider expert review when artefact, incomplete myocardial nulling or unusual distribution makes interpretation uncertain. Diffuse interstitial disease may be better characterized using mapping and extracellular-volume assessment than focal enhancement alone. Conversely, absent LGE does not exclude every genetic cardiomyopathy or an early inflammatory process. Select further testing to answer a defined question, such as genetic cause, active inflammation or an infiltrative subtype, rather than treating the scan as a replacement for the whole diagnostic pathway.')],['CMP2023','MYO2025','CMRCONSENSUS'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Autosomal Dominant Cardiomyopathy and Penetrance',
 'A heterozygous parent has a confirmed pathogenic autosomal dominant sarcomeric variant. The other parent does not carry it. Which statement correctly describes counseling for each child?',
 'Each child has a fifty-percent transmission probability, while clinical expression and severity remain variable',[
 ('Each child has a twenty-five-percent transmission probability because the disorder may be silent','Twenty-five-percent affected risk describes a typical autosomal recessive carrier-pair situation. Reduced penetrance changes expression after inheritance, not the one-half transmission probability from this heterozygous parent.'),
 ('Each child inherits the variant, but only half develop disease','That confuses transmission with penetrance. One-half transmission applies to each pregnancy; penetrance among carriers is a separate, age- and variant-dependent question.'),
 ('Only children with an abnormal ECG can have inherited the variant','Clinical manifestations can be delayed or incomplete. A normal ECG cannot establish absence of a known familial pathogenic variant in an otherwise at-risk child.')],
 [S('Distinguish Transmission, Penetrance and Expressivity','**A heterozygous autosomal dominant variant has a one-half probability of transmission in each pregnancy.** Previous children’s results do not change that independent probability.',
    'Penetrance describes whether a carrier manifests the phenotype, often in an age-dependent manner. Expressivity describes the form and severity of manifestations among affected carriers.',
    'The same familial variant can be associated with different wall thickness, conduction disease, ventricular function or arrhythmic history. A parent’s current mild phenotype does not guarantee a similarly mild course for every carrier child.'),
  S('Use Counseling to Guide Testing and Surveillance','**Offer appropriately counseled targeted testing when a reliable familial pathogenic or likely pathogenic result is available.** Timing in children depends on the disease, family presentation and whether results change childhood surveillance or care.',
    'A genotype-positive relative with normal initial imaging can need continued phenotype surveillance. A negative result for the established familial cause has a different meaning from a negative broad panel in an affected proband.',
    'Do not assume all cardiomyopathies follow autosomal dominant inheritance. X-linked, recessive, mitochondrial and syndromic conditions require their own counseling. Review the validity of the gene–disease association and the variant classification before giving predictive advice. Discuss reproductive choices and family communication without predicting inevitable disability from genotype alone. New symptoms require evaluation even during a scheduled surveillance interval, and variant interpretation may need review as evidence changes. Clinical care follows the relevant phenotype and risk rather than transmission probability alone.')],['CMP2023','GEN2022'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'A Variant of Uncertain Significance in Cardiomyopathy',
 'A DCM proband has a variant of uncertain significance in a plausible disease gene. An asymptomatic sister asks for a predictive test so a negative result can end cardiac surveillance. Which response is most appropriate?',
 'Do not use the VUS as a definitive predictive test; arrange clinical family screening and specialist variant review',[
 ('Use targeted VUS testing and discharge the sister if negative','Predictive exclusion requires a sufficiently established familial causal variant. Absence of an uncertain finding does not reliably exclude the unknown familial cause of this DCM.'),
 ('Diagnose cardiomyopathy in the sister if the VUS is present','Presence of a VUS does not establish pathogenicity or a clinical phenotype. Overclassification can lead to unnecessary restrictions, treatment and anxiety.'),
 ('Treat the VUS as benign and end evaluation of the whole family','Uncertain is not equivalent to benign. The proband’s disease and pedigree can still justify clinical family screening while the molecular finding remains unresolved.')],
 [S('Uncertainty Is a Classification With Clinical Consequences','**A VUS is not a pathogenic or likely pathogenic result.** A plausible gene name and an uncommon DNA change are insufficient to establish that the variant explains the cardiomyopathy.',
    'Review the gene–disease validity, phenotype fit, population frequency and available segregation or functional evidence through an experienced cardiovascular genetics service. Do not independently upgrade the report based on one suggestive clinical similarity.',
    'Testing selected informative affected relatives can help determine segregation and eventual reclassification. This is different from using the finding as a validated predictive test in a healthy relative.'),
  S('Continue Phenotype-Based Family Care','**Clinical screening can remain indicated because the familial cause is unresolved.** History, examination, ECG and cardiac imaging assess disease rather than the uncertain molecular label.',
    'A normal initial evaluation does not necessarily eliminate future expression in an at-risk family. Follow-up depends on age, phenotype, family history and the specialist plan.',
    'Arrange a mechanism for updated interpretation rather than leaving the VUS permanently treated as either disease-causing or harmless. If it is later reclassified with adequate evidence, appropriately counseled cascade testing may become informative. ICD treatment, drug prescriptions and exercise restrictions should not be triggered solely by VUS carriage. Conversely, an affected sister would require clinical care even if she lacked this uncertain finding. Explain why a reassuring-looking targeted result cannot answer the family’s risk question until the variant’s causal role is established.')],['CMP2023','GEN2022'])

add(SECTIONS[0][0],'Interpreting a Negative Cascade Genetic Test',
 'A familial pathogenic variant convincingly explains the proband’s autosomal dominant cardiomyopathy. An adult first-degree relative has a normal clinical assessment and tests negative for that specific variant. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'End routine surveillance for the established familial variant, with return for symptoms or revised family information',[
 ('Continue the same serial screening used for a genotype-positive relative','Continued surveillance is appropriate for a carrier or when familial causality remains unresolved. Here the established cause is excluded and the clinical assessment is normal, so retaining the carrier schedule is generally unnecessary.'),
 ('Order a broad cardiomyopathy panel before interpreting the targeted negative','Broad testing can be useful when an unexplained phenotype or complex family evidence remains. It is not routinely needed to validate an informative negative in this reassuring relative and can introduce uncertain findings.'),
 ('Discharge the relative but retain familial-variant surveillance for all their children','A person who does not carry the established familial variant cannot transmit it. Routine surveillance of descendants for that same familial cause is generally unnecessary unless another clinical or family concern emerges.')],
 [S('Ask Whether the Negative Result Is Informative','**Targeted cascade testing is most useful after identifying a convincing familial pathogenic or likely pathogenic variant.** In an otherwise reassuring relative, absence of that cause can remove the need for repeated surveillance directed at it.',
    'Confirm that the family’s disease is adequately explained and that no additional causal variant or separate phenotype is known. Complex pedigrees, an affected test-negative relative or uncertain causality require reassessment rather than automatic discharge.',
    'Do not extend the same conclusion to a VUS or to an affected proband with no finding on a broad panel. Current testing does not detect every genetic mechanism.'),
  S('Combine Molecular Results With Clinical Findings','**An abnormal clinical phenotype needs investigation even when targeted familial testing is negative.** Another cause or a more complex familial explanation may be present.',
    'A genotype-positive, phenotype-negative relative has the converse problem: clinical expression may emerge later, so disease-specific surveillance can remain necessary. Neither group should be managed by family history alone without interpreting the actual result.',
    'Document the result and the reason for the follow-up plan, and advise review for syncope, exertional symptoms, sustained palpitations or new family information. Discuss implications for descendants with genetic counseling; a relative who does not carry the variant cannot transmit that variant through the tested family line. This is a specific statement about the established familial cause, not a guarantee against coronary disease, hypertension or other future cardiac problems. Revisit the plan if the original molecular classification changes.')],['CMP2023','GEN2022'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Adult Diagnostic Wall-Thickness Thresholds in HCM',
 'An adult first-degree relative of a patient with unequivocal HCM has maximal end-diastolic LV wall thickness 14 mm, measured reliably. There is no hypertension, valve disease or other adequate loading explanation. Which interpretation fits ESC 2023 diagnostic criteria?',
 'The familial adult wall-thickness threshold is met; fifteen millimeters is not required in this context',[
 ('HCM is excluded because every adult requires at least fifteen millimeters','The usual unexplained adult threshold is fifteen millimeters, but adult first-degree relatives of unequivocally affected patients have a lower thirteen-millimeter threshold in the specified framework.'),
 ('HCM requires resting outflow obstruction in addition to the wall thickness','Obstruction is a functional subclassification, not a requirement for HCM diagnosis. Many affected patients have nonobstructive disease or develop obstruction only with provocation.'),
 ('HCM requires a positive genetic test in addition to the familial imaging finding','Genetics can clarify cause and family testing, but clinical diagnostic criteria do not require finding a pathogenic variant in every otherwise qualifying affected relative.')],
 [S('Interpret Thickness in the Appropriate Population','**ESC defines adult HCM by maximal LV wall thickness of at least 15 mm not explained solely by loading conditions.** In adult first-degree relatives of unequivocal disease, at least 13 mm can establish the clinical hypertrophic phenotype.',
    'A borderline measurement outside that familial setting needs additional evidence, such as the pedigree, ECG, molecular findings and morphology. Do not interpret thirteen millimeters as a universal threshold for every adult.',
    'Pediatric assessment uses body-size-adjusted measurements and the appropriate clinical framework. Applying an unadjusted adult millimeter cut-off to a small child can misclassify disease.'),
  S('Confirm Measurement and Exclude Phenocopies','**Measure maximal end-diastolic thickness across the LV rather than assuming the basal septum is the only relevant segment.** CMR can resolve poor echo windows and identify apical or other focal hypertrophy.',
    'Exclude inclusion of RV structures, trabeculae or oblique imaging in the measurement. Hypertension can coexist with HCM, so the question is whether loading conditions adequately explain the magnitude and pattern.',
    'Evaluate amyloid, Fabry and other storage or syndromic conditions when clinical clues suggest them; unexplained thickness is a phenotype, not proof of a sarcomere defect. Once HCM is supported, assess symptoms, LVOT gradients, atrial arrhythmia and sudden-death risk rather than basing treatment solely on the diagnostic threshold. Family genetic counseling and appropriate screening remain relevant even when the proband’s panel is negative. A fourteen-millimeter familial diagnosis does not independently establish an indication for septal reduction or an ICD.')],['CMP2023','HCM2024'],style='direct',
 table=(['Adult setting under ESC 2023','Wall-thickness interpretation'],[
 ['Unexplained hypertrophy in an adult','≥15 mm in any LV myocardial segment'],
 ['First-degree relative of unequivocal HCM','≥13 mm can meet the familial criterion'],
 ['Borderline thickness outside that context','Integrate loading conditions, family, ECG and genetic evidence']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'LV Outflow Obstruction: Definition and Treatment Threshold',
 'A patient with established HCM has a resting peak instantaneous LVOT gradient of 36 mmHg and a provoked gradient of 43 mmHg. Which interpretation correctly separates classification from the threshold usually used to consider advanced obstruction-directed treatment?',
 'Resting obstructive HCM, below the usual gradient threshold for advanced treatment assessment',[
 ('Nonobstructive HCM, because the maximum documented gradient is below fifty','The definition of obstruction uses thirty millimeters of mercury, not fifty. A resting gradient of thirty-six meets that definition even though it is below the usual advanced-treatment threshold.'),
 ('Resting obstructive HCM, already meeting the usual gradient criterion for septal reduction','Resting obstruction is correctly recognized, but the usual intervention gradient is at least fifty with relevant symptoms and suitable anatomy. Neither supplied measurement reaches that hemodynamic threshold.'),
 ('Latent obstructive HCM, because only the provoked measurement qualifies as obstruction','Latent obstruction describes a qualifying provoked gradient when resting obstruction is absent. Here the resting value already exceeds thirty, so the disease is obstructive at rest.')],
 [S('Use Two Thresholds for Two Different Questions','**A peak instantaneous Doppler LVOT gradient of at least 30 mmHg at rest or with physiological provocation defines obstruction.** A gradient of at least 50 mmHg is generally the level used when considering advanced drugs or septal reduction for persistent attributable symptoms.',
    'The gradient is dynamic. Preload, afterload, contractility, heart rate and activity can change its magnitude, so one resting value does not fully describe an individual’s exertional physiology.',
    'Document the measurement conditions and examine systolic anterior mitral motion and associated mitral regurgitation. Avoid mistaking a high-velocity MR jet for the outflow signal.'),
  S('Link Hemodynamics to Symptoms and Treatment','**Neither numerical threshold alone establishes an indication for an invasive procedure.** Assess symptom burden, the relationship to obstruction, prior therapy, anatomy and patient preferences.',
    'Physiological provocation and exercise echocardiography can reveal clinically relevant latent obstruction when resting or simple provoked gradients are below fifty. Doppler gradients should not be substituted directly for a catheter peak-to-peak measurement.',
    'Initial symptomatic obstructive management commonly uses a nonvasodilating beta blocker, with suitable alternatives and escalation assessed individually. Persistent symptoms with adequate obstruction can prompt consideration of a myosin inhibitor, disopyramide or septal reduction in an experienced service. If symptoms appear disproportionate to a modest gradient, investigate diastolic dysfunction, ischemia, AF, pulmonary disease and other contributors rather than adding rest and provoked numbers. Repeat assessment under relevant conditions when symptoms change. This patient meets the definition of obstruction but the supplied measurements do not by themselves meet the usual advanced-treatment gradient threshold.')],['CMP2023','HCM2024'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Exercise Echocardiography for Latent LVOT Obstruction',
 'A patient with confirmed HCM has reproducible exertional dyspnea and near-syncope. Resting LVOT gradient is 18 mmHg and Valsalva produces 28 mmHg. There is no acute illness or unstable rhythm. Which investigation best assesses whether dynamic obstruction explains the exertional symptoms?',
 'Supervised exercise echocardiography with LVOT assessment',[
 ('Dobutamine stress echocardiography','Dobutamine can provoke obstruction through nonphysiological inotropic stimulation. Exercise is the preferred physiological approach for this symptom–gradient question rather than using an artificial drug-induced peak as the clinical surrogate.'),
 ('Resting contrast-enhanced CMR','CMR characterizes morphology, scar and alternative diagnoses, but a resting study does not reproduce the dynamic loading and contractile conditions causing the supplied exertional symptoms.'),
 ('Coronary CT angiography','Coronary CT can investigate suspected coronary disease in an appropriate patient. It does not replace Doppler assessment of the changing outflow gradient during physiological exercise.')],
 [S('Reproduce the Relevant Physiology','**Exercise echocardiography is recommended in symptomatic HCM when resting and simple provoked testing do not reveal a gradient of at least 50 mmHg.** It can identify latent obstruction and relate it to the patient’s symptoms.',
    'Standing and Valsalva are useful initial provocative maneuvers, but a negative or modest response does not exclude exercise-associated obstruction. Dynamic gradients change with preload, afterload, heart rate and contractility.',
    'Record the gradient under the protocol’s exercise and recovery conditions, symptoms, blood-pressure response and rhythm. Interpretation should distinguish the LVOT signal from mitral regurgitation and identify the mechanism of obstruction.'),
  S('Choose Each Test for the Question It Can Answer','**A physiological provoked gradient is interpreted alongside symptoms and anatomy.** It is not interchangeable with an isolated pharmacological response or proof that obstruction explains every complaint.',
    'CMR remains valuable for poorly visualized hypertrophy, apical disease, scar and risk assessment. Coronary evaluation is appropriate when ischemic symptoms, age or risk factors create that separate question.',
    'Use supervision and rescue capability appropriate to the patient’s history; exercise testing is not an unsupervised challenge for someone with unstable symptoms. Persistent exertional limitation without a significant gradient warrants assessment of diastolic dysfunction, microvascular ischemia, AF, chronotropic limitation, pulmonary disease or other contributors. A newly demonstrated gradient of at least fifty can support advanced obstruction-directed assessment when symptoms remain important despite suitable treatment, but does not independently establish a septal procedure indication. Document both the resting and provoked results so later treatment decisions reflect the actual hemodynamic context.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Athletic Remodeling Versus Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy',
 'A competitive endurance athlete has borderline LV wall thickening on screening. Which additional finding would most strongly increase concern for HCM rather than uncomplicated athletic remodeling?',
 'A concordant family history with focal asymmetric hypertrophy and nonischemic scar',[
 ('Balanced biventricular enlargement with preserved functional reserve','Proportionate enlargement and an appropriate exercise response can accompany endurance adaptation. They do not exclude disease by themselves, but are less concerning than the convergent familial and focal tissue findings.'),
 ('Regression of wall thickening after an adequate period of detraining','Reversal supports a training-related component. Lack of complete regression is not a standalone HCM criterion, but documented regression is less supportive of fixed pathological hypertrophy than the proposed familial scar phenotype.'),
 ('Isolated sinus bradycardia with otherwise reassuring examination and imaging','Resting bradycardia can reflect athletic autonomic adaptation. Without a concerning electrical, structural or family context it is a weaker disease marker than focal hypertrophy with scar and an affected pedigree.')],
 [S('Use Convergent Evidence in the Borderline Zone','**Borderline wall thickness in an athlete requires integrated assessment rather than a single imaging cut-off.** Training type, sex, body size, ancestry, loading conditions and measurement quality affect interpretation.',
    'A concerning pedigree, focal or asymmetric hypertrophy, impaired relaxation, unexpected ECG abnormalities and disease-pattern scar increase suspicion. No single finding has perfect sensitivity or specificity.',
    'Physiological endurance remodeling often includes proportionate chamber enlargement and preserved reserve. However, athletic participation does not prevent inherited disease, and an athletic-looking chamber does not overrule a strongly abnormal family or tissue assessment.'),
  S('Resolve Uncertainty Before Giving Definitive Advice','**CMR can clarify morphology, apical involvement, scar and alternative diagnoses.** Review the complete ECG and consider appropriate ambulatory and exercise assessment rather than labeling every repolarization variant pathological.',
    'Detraining can provide supportive information when feasible, but neither a short incomplete trial nor absent regression alone proves HCM. Interpret a pathogenic molecular finding through the phenotype; a VUS is not diagnostic.',
    'Counsel through an experienced sports-cardiology or HCM service when the distinction remains uncertain. If HCM is established, participation decisions require current individualized risk assessment and shared discussion, rather than an automatic assumption that all exercise is prohibited. If evaluation supports physiological adaptation, explain the evidence and any follow-up triggers. Syncope, new exertional symptoms, progressive structural change or new familial information should prompt reassessment. The supplied answer is strongest because independent familial, morphological and tissue findings converge, not because asymmetry or any small enhancement focus alone conclusively identifies a sarcomeric disorder.')],['CMP2023','HCM2024'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Imaging Suspected Apical Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy',
 'An adult has marked precordial T-wave inversion and persistent clinical suspicion of apical HCM. Routine echo shows preserved EF but the apex is foreshortened and poorly defined. CMR is feasible. Which investigation most directly resolves the remaining structural question?',
 'CMR with complete apical morphology and tissue characterization',[
 ('Transesophageal echocardiography','TEE is useful for valve anatomy and selected structural questions. The LV apex is relatively far from the probe, so it is not the preferred comprehensive test for this incompletely defined apical phenotype when CMR is feasible.'),
 ('Invasive left ventriculography','Ventriculography can demonstrate cavity shape when catheterization is otherwise indicated. It adds procedural burden and does not provide the myocardial thickness and scar characterization available from CMR.'),
 ('Exercise stress echocardiography','Exercise echo is important for latent obstruction and symptom physiology. It does not reliably solve an inadequate apical acoustic window or provide the same tissue characterization as CMR.')],
 [S('Inspect the Entire Ventricle','**Apical HCM can be missed when the apex is foreshortened or inadequately visualized.** Preserved global EF and a normal basal septal measurement do not exclude regional hypertrophy.',
    'CMR defines apical thickness, cavity shape, scar and possible aneurysm or thrombus. Compare measurements in correctly aligned end-diastolic views rather than including trabeculae or an oblique section.',
    'Marked T-wave inversion is a useful clue but not a unique diagnosis. Coronary disease, other cardiomyopathies and relevant electrical or systemic causes must remain in the differential according to the presentation.'),
  S('Use the Result to Assess More Than the Label','**An ultrasound-enhancing agent is a reasonable echo alternative when apical definition is inadequate, particularly if CMR is unavailable or contraindicated.** The question supplies feasible CMR, making it a direct comprehensive structural investigation.',
    'Look for midventricular obstruction and an apical aneurysm; an aneurysm can create a distinct arrhythmic and thromboembolic problem. Apical phenotype alone should not be equated with universally benign prognosis.',
    'Assess symptoms, rhythm, family history and the full HCM sudden-death framework after confirming anatomy. Treat a documented thrombus according to the indication, but do not assume every apical cavity shape requires anticoagulation. CMR complements echocardiographic hemodynamics and ambulatory rhythm assessment rather than replacing them. An enhancement pattern adds prognostic context but is not by itself proof of a particular causal variant. Explain why an apparently reassuring EF does not resolve a regional anatomical question, and ensure the eventual report describes the apex explicitly.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

R['HCMCASE2024']=('ACC clinical teaching case: HCM medication dosing, 2024','https://www.acc.org/education-and-meetings/patient-case-quizzes/2024/10/23/10/29/how-to-manage-myosin-inhibitors')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Initial Medication for Symptomatic Obstructive HCM',
 'A patient with HCM has exertional dyspnea attributable to a resting LVOT gradient of 72 mmHg. BP is 126/76 mmHg, EF is preserved, and there is no congestion, bronchospasm, marked bradycardia or AV block. No symptom-directed medication has been tried. Which initial strategy best follows the AHA/ACC 2024 pathway?',
 'Titrate a nonvasodilating beta blocker to symptom benefit and tolerance',[
 ('Start verapamil as initial monotherapy','Verapamil is an appropriate alternative when a beta blocker is ineffective or not tolerated. In the named 2024 pathway, the suitable untreated patient first receives a nonvasodilating beta blocker.'),
 ('Start disopyramide with AV nodal blockade as the initial regimen','This is an established escalation strategy for persistent obstructive symptoms. QT, renal and anticholinergic effects require assessment; it is not the first step in the specified untreated 2024-pathway presentation.'),
 ('Start a cardiac myosin inhibitor as the initial regimen','Myosin inhibitors are effective treatment options, and newer monotherapy evidence has developed. The question explicitly asks for the 2024 pathway, which starts with an appropriate nonvasodilating beta blocker before its escalation choices.')],
 [S('Reduce the Dynamic Obstructive Burden','**A nonvasodilating beta blocker is recommended for symptoms attributable to obstructive HCM.** Reduced adrenergic drive, slower rate and reduced contractility can improve filling and lessen obstruction.',
    'Choose a suitable agent and titrate according to symptoms, pressure, heart rate, conduction and tolerance rather than treating a single resting pulse as the whole therapeutic endpoint. Review bronchospasm, advanced conduction disease and interactions.',
    'The aim is symptom and physiological improvement; this prescription should not be presented as a proven replacement for indicated sudden-death protection.'),
  S('Use Alternatives and Escalation With Safety Checks','**Verapamil or diltiazem can replace an ineffective or poorly tolerated beta blocker in suitable patients.** Avoid assuming that combining both nodal drug classes gives greater benefit without additional risk.',
    'Verapamil can be harmful with hypotension, severe dyspnea at rest or very high resting gradients, particularly above one hundred millimeters of mercury. Reduced EF and conduction disease also change the drug balance.',
    'Persistent important symptoms despite suitable first-line therapy warrant an experienced HCM assessment for a cardiac myosin inhibitor in an eligible adult, disopyramide with appropriate nodal blockade, or septal reduction. Diuretics can be used cautiously for genuine congestion, but excessive preload reduction may worsen obstruction; this stem supplies no congestion. Review medicines that lower afterload or increase contractility, hydration and triggers. Reassess symptoms and gradients after titration rather than escalating solely because the original gradient was high. A practical adult starting example is metoprolol succinate extended release 25 mg orally once daily, followed by monitored titration to symptom benefit and tolerance. This is a formulation-specific example, not a universal dose for every beta blocker.')],['HCM2024', 'CMP2023', 'HCMCASE2024'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Acute Hypotension With Dynamic LVOT Obstruction',
 'A patient with known obstructive HCM becomes hypotensive after volume depletion. Bedside echo shows a small hyperdynamic LV, systolic anterior mitral motion and a severe dynamic LVOT gradient; lungs are clear. After cautious IV fluid assessment and replacement, hypotension persists. Which vasoactive strategy is most appropriate?',
 'A monitored vasoconstrictor without beta-inotropic activity, such as phenylephrine',[
 ('Dobutamine to increase contractility of the hyperdynamic LV','Dobutamine can support low-output systolic pump failure in selected settings. Here additional inotropy can intensify systolic anterior motion and dynamic obstruction, worsening forward flow.'),
 ('Nitroprusside to reduce the pressure against which the LV ejects','Afterload reduction can improve selected severe regurgitant or hypertensive presentations. In this preload-depleted obstructive physiology it can worsen the outflow gradient and hypotension.'),
 ('IV loop diuretic escalation to shrink the hyperdynamic cavity','Diuresis treats congestion when it is present. Further reducing preload in a small underfilled LV with clear lungs can aggravate obstruction rather than correct the mechanism of shock.')],
 [S('Identify the Mechanism Before Choosing Shock Treatment','**Dynamic LVOTO can cause low forward output despite a high EF.** Volume depletion, vasodilation, tachycardia and increased contractility can narrow the outflow region and intensify systolic anterior mitral motion.',
    'Assess bleeding, infection, arrhythmia, ischemia and other causes concurrently. A known HCM diagnosis does not prove that every hypotensive episode is due to obstruction; the supplied echo identifies the mechanism here.',
    'Replace a genuine volume deficit cautiously while monitoring lungs and filling. Fluid is not an unlimited prescription when congestion or another shock mechanism is present.'),
  S('Support Filling and Afterload Without Increasing Inotropy','**AHA/ACC guidance recommends phenylephrine or another vasoconstrictor without inotropic activity when acute hypotension persists despite fluid administration.** Titrate under continuous hemodynamic monitoring.',
    'Specialist beta blockade can sometimes be used with vasoconstrictor support to reduce contractility and obstruction. Do not extrapolate this into unmonitored beta-blocker administration in every shocked patient.',
    'Review and withhold aggravating vasodilators or inotropes when clinically appropriate. Correct the precipitating volume loss and rhythm problem, then repeat echo and reassess perfusion rather than judging success from blood pressure alone. This differs from severe primary systolic pump failure, where selected inotropic support may be necessary. Mitral regurgitation caused by systolic anterior motion can improve as the obstructive physiology is corrected. The drug choice is determined by the demonstrated dynamic mechanism, not simply by the words cardiogenic shock. Escalate to an experienced critical-care and HCM team when perfusion remains inadequate or the diagnosis is mixed.')],['HCM2024'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Escalation for Persistent Obstructive HCM Symptoms',
 'An adult with obstructive HCM remains markedly limited by exertional dyspnea despite an adequately titrated nonvasodilating beta blocker and an appropriate alternative-drug assessment. Physiological provocation produces an 86-mmHg gradient. EF is preserved. Under AHA/ACC 2024 guidance, which next strategy is appropriate?',
 'Shared selection among myosin inhibition, disopyramide-based treatment and expert septal reduction',[
 ('Require failure of disopyramide before discussing any myosin inhibitor or septal procedure','Disopyramide is a legitimate escalation option, but the named pathway does not require every patient to exhaust it before a shared discussion of the other supported alternatives.'),
 ('Choose alcohol septal ablation before assessing septal and coronary anatomy','Alcohol ablation is appropriate in selected anatomy and clinical circumstances. Its safety and efficacy depend on a suitable target and broader procedural assessment; a high gradient alone does not establish that choice.'),
 ('Add verapamil to the maximally tolerated beta blocker as the default escalation','Verapamil can replace a poorly tolerated or ineffective beta blocker in suitable patients. Routine dual nodal/negative-inotropic treatment is not the named default escalation and can cause bradycardia, conduction block and hypotension.')],
 [S('Confirm That Obstruction Explains the Limitation','**Persistent symptoms despite appropriate initial therapy and a significant resting or physiologically provoked gradient support advanced HCM assessment.** Recheck adherence, dose tolerance, loading conditions and competing causes of dyspnea.',
    'The supplied gradient is above the usual fifty-millimeter threshold. Imaging must still define septal thickness, mitral anatomy, systolic anterior motion and the location of obstruction.',
    'Discuss the patient’s goals, comorbidity, monitoring access and procedural preferences. A numerical gradient alone does not decide between medication and intervention.'),
  S('Choose Among Supported Alternatives Rather Than a Mandatory Sequence','**The 2024 US pathway includes an eligible adult cardiac myosin inhibitor, disopyramide with AV nodal blockade, or septal reduction in an experienced service.** The balance depends on anatomy, contraindications, availability and preference.',
    'Myosin inhibition requires EF surveillance and interaction review because excess negative inotropy can cause systolic dysfunction. Disopyramide requires QT, renal and anticholinergic assessment and appropriate nodal protection.',
    'Myectomy can address anatomy that also requires mitral or other cardiac surgery. Alcohol ablation requires suitable septal arterial supply and is selected after procedural risk assessment, not from age or gradient alone. Explain the possibility of conduction block, pacemaker need and other procedural complications. Symptom improvement does not remove an independent ICD or AF-anticoagulation indication. Use the recommendation year explicitly; evolving drug approvals and labels need their own current checks before prescribing. Reassess the outcome after the chosen strategy rather than treating referral as the endpoint of care.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

R['MAVA2026']=('Mavacamten US prescribing information, revised September 2026','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2026/214998s013lbl.pdf')
R['AFICALABEL']=('Aficamten US prescribing information, 2025','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/219083s000lbl.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Mavacamten and New Systolic Dysfunction',
 'An adult taking mavacamten for symptomatic obstructive HCM reports improved exercise tolerance. Surveillance echo shows LVEF 48%, down from 66%, with a much lower outflow gradient. There are no overt HF symptoms. Under the September 2026 US label, what is the appropriate immediate drug action?',
 'Interrupt mavacamten and reassess ventricular function through the treatment-interruption pathway',[
 ('Continue the same dose because symptoms and the gradient have improved','Gradient reduction is useful, but it does not override the EF safety threshold. The interruption rule applies even when the patient has not yet developed overt HF symptoms.'),
 ('Reduce the dose while continuing treatment until the next routine visit','Dose adjustment can be appropriate in other monitoring situations. At an EF below fifty percent the label calls for interruption, not simply continued exposure at a smaller dose.'),
 ('Permanently discontinue mavacamten at this first EF decline','Permanent discontinuation may become appropriate under the toxicity or recurrent-event pathway. The supplied first asymptomatic decline calls for interruption and reassessment rather than assuming that future monitored treatment is excluded.')],
 [S('Separate Hemodynamic Benefit From Excessive Contractile Suppression','**Myosin inhibition reduces excessive actin–myosin interaction in obstructive HCM.** Reduced force generation can lessen dynamic obstruction, while too much contractile suppression can create a different clinical problem.',
    'A lower gradient is not sufficient evidence of safe treatment. Assess ventricular function, perfusion, symptoms, rhythm and the wider clinical course rather than optimizing one Doppler value in isolation.',
    'Consider an intercurrent illness, arrhythmia, ischemia or other contributor to a new EF reduction as well as drug effect. Improved exertional symptoms do not establish that an unexpected imaging change is harmless.'),
  S('Apply the Current Label and Review the Whole Regimen','**The September 2026 US label directs interruption below LVEF 50%; initiation or up-titration below 55% is not recommended.** The usual adult starting dose is 5 mg daily, with label-guided titration up to 15 mg daily.',
    'Review CYP interactions and additive negative inotropes. Strong CYP2C19 inhibitors and moderate/strong CYP2C19 or CYP3A4 inducers are contraindicated. Pregnancy prevention and serial echo are required safety considerations.',
    'The updated US indication includes symptomatic obstructive disease in adults and pediatric patients weighing at least 30 kg; this differs from the adult-only myosin-inhibitor wording in the 2024 guideline. Follow the current interruption/restart algorithm rather than extrapolating from the initial prescription. Obstruction-directed medication does not replace separate assessment of ventricular arrhythmia or AF-related embolic risk. Coordinate treatment changes with the HCM service and review all prescription and nonprescription medicines. A favorable symptomatic response is a reason to preserve a useful treatment safely if feasible, not a reason to ignore the monitoring result.'),
  S('Do Not Substitute Another Myosin Inhibitor’s Algorithm','**Aficamten has a different US-labeled EF response algorithm.** Its usual adult initiation is 5 mg daily; initiation below EF 55% is not recommended.',
    'The aficamten label calls for dose reduction when EF is below 50% but at least 40%, and interruption below 40% or with worsening clinical status. Those thresholds must not be used to continue mavacamten in this question.',
    'Both agents require echo-based safety monitoring and medication-interaction review, but their dose steps and interruption rules are drug-specific. A class-level mechanism does not make the labels interchangeable.')],['HCM2024','MAVA2026','AFICALABEL'])

R['EXPLORER']=('EXPLORER-HCM randomized trial, 2020','https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(20)31792-X')
R['SEQUOIA']=('SEQUOIA-HCM randomized trial, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2401424')
R['MAPLE']=('MAPLE-HCM aficamten versus metoprolol monotherapy trial, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2504654')
add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Interpreting Myosin-Inhibitor Functional Trials',
 'Which statement accurately describes the primary efficacy result of EXPLORER-HCM?',
 'Mavacamten improved the prespecified exercise-capacity/NYHA composite at thirty weeks compared with placebo',[
 ('Mavacamten reduced all-cause mortality compared with placebo as the primary endpoint','The primary endpoint assessed functional response, not mortality. A short-term improvement in exercise and symptoms cannot be relabeled as a demonstrated survival benefit.'),
 ('Mavacamten was directly superior to surgical myectomy for the primary endpoint','The randomized comparator was placebo, not myectomy. A decision between drug treatment and a procedure must not be represented as settled by a head-to-head comparison that was not performed.'),
 ('Mavacamten established primary-endpoint benefit in nonobstructive HCM','EXPLORER-HCM enrolled symptomatic obstructive disease. Results cannot simply be transferred to a different phenotype without its own evidence.')],
 [S('Know the Population, Comparator and Endpoint','**EXPLORER-HCM randomized 251 adults with symptomatic obstructive HCM to mavacamten or placebo.** At thirty weeks, the primary functional response occurred in 37% versus 17%.',
    'Response required either peak oxygen uptake increasing by at least 1.5 mL/kg/min together with at least one NYHA-class improvement, or an increase of at least 3.0 mL/kg/min without NYHA worsening.',
    'This composite combines objective exercise performance with clinical functional class. It should not be described as a mortality endpoint, universal abolition of obstruction, or proof that every treated patient improves.'),
  S('Distinguish Related Trials and the Clinical Decision','**SEQUOIA-HCM tested aficamten against placebo in symptomatic obstructive HCM.** Its twenty-four-week primary endpoint was change in peak oxygen uptake, with a between-group difference of 1.7 mL/kg/min, 95% CI 1.0–2.4.',
    'Different molecules, dose protocols, endpoints and follow-up periods prevent a simple cross-trial ranking. Neither comparison is a randomized demonstration that one drug is superior to the other or to septal myectomy.',
    'Functional improvement supports a treatment option for a suitable symptomatic patient, but monitoring, interactions, contraindications and personal preference remain part of the choice. Evaluate persistent limitation after treatment using symptoms, exercise performance and hemodynamics rather than assuming a lower resting gradient is a complete response. Procedural alternatives remain relevant when anatomy or accompanying surgical disease favors them. Trial efficacy does not remove independent indications for an ICD, anticoagulation or management of ventricular dysfunction. Distinguish a trial result from a regulatory indication: current labeling and local access require their own verification. The central examination task is to identify what the randomized study actually measured before turning its result into a management claim.'),
  S('Place Newer Monotherapy Evidence in Context','**MAPLE-HCM is a separate 2025 randomized comparison of aficamten with metoprolol monotherapy.** It used titrated daily doses of 5–20 mg aficamten or 50–200 mg metoprolol.',
    'At twenty-four weeks, peak oxygen uptake changed by +1.1 mL/kg/min with aficamten and −1.2 with metoprolol; the between-group difference was 2.3 mL/kg/min, 95% CI 1.5–3.1, P<0.001. Unlike EXPLORER and SEQUOIA, this is an active-comparator result, not a placebo comparison or a mortality endpoint.',
    'Keep trial protocols, current drug labels and the dated 2024 guideline sequence distinct. Evidence generated after a guideline can expand clinical discussion without rewriting what that guideline originally recommended.')],['EXPLORER','SEQUOIA','HCM2024','MAPLE'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Selecting Septal Myectomy When Other Surgery Is Needed',
 'A suitable surgical candidate has severely symptomatic obstructive HCM despite appropriate medication, a 92-mmHg provoked gradient and intrinsic degenerative mitral disease requiring repair. Which procedural strategy best addresses the supplied anatomy?',
 'Septal myectomy with appropriate mitral repair at an experienced HCM surgical center',[
 ('Alcohol septal ablation followed by reassessment of the intrinsic mitral lesion','Alcohol ablation can relieve obstruction in selected patients, especially when surgery is unsuitable. It does not repair an intrinsic degenerative valve lesion already requiring surgery, making a combined surgical approach more appropriate here.'),
 ('Mitral repair without a septal procedure','Repair treats the intrinsic lesion, but does not reliably eliminate the separate septal mechanism producing severe LVOTO. Planning should address both anatomical contributors rather than assuming valve repair alone solves the outflow problem.'),
 ('Mitral valve replacement without septal reduction','Replacement can be needed for genuinely nonrepairable intrinsic valve disease. Here repair is feasible and significant septal obstruction is present; replacing the valve to address outflow physiology is less suitable than tailored myectomy with repair.')],
 [S('Match the Intervention to All Relevant Anatomy','**Myectomy is favored when obstructive HCM coexists with cardiac disease that independently requires surgery.** Intrinsic mitral disease, abnormal papillary anatomy, significant coronary disease or another surgical valve lesion can make a combined operation appropriate.',
    'Distinguish intrinsic valve pathology from regurgitation caused mainly by systolic anterior motion. The latter may improve after adequate relief of obstruction without a separate valve replacement.',
    'Review septal thickness, obstruction location, mitral apparatus and coronary anatomy through an experienced multidisciplinary team. Intraoperative imaging helps assess the result and residual mitral dysfunction.'),
  S('Compare Septal Reduction Strategies','**Alcohol ablation requires a suitable septal arterial target and appropriate myocardial distribution.** It creates a controlled septal infarct and cannot correct every accompanying structural abnormality.',
    'It is particularly relevant when severe symptoms persist and surgery is contraindicated or carries unacceptable risk, provided anatomy is suitable. Discuss heart block and pacemaker need as well as residual obstruction and repeat intervention.',
    'Septal reduction requires important attributable symptoms, adequate dynamic obstruction and sufficient target anatomy. Neither a gradient alone nor a wish to prevent sudden death establishes the procedure indication. Do not replace a mitral valve solely to relieve LVOTO when an appropriate septal procedure can address the mechanism. Continue independent AF, HF and arrhythmic-risk assessment after successful surgery; symptom relief is not evidence that every disease-related risk has disappeared. The supplied valve lesion is a separate reason to choose a surgical strategy, rather than treating all high-gradient patients as interchangeable candidates for catheter ablation.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Major Adult HCM Sudden-Death Risk Markers',
 'An adult with HCM has no previous arrest or sustained VT. Which finding is a major primary-prevention risk marker in the AHA/ACC 2024 framework?',
 'An LV apical aneurysm with transmural scar or LGE',[
 ('LGE involving eighteen percent of LV mass','Extensive LGE can support ICD consideration when assessment remains uncertain, but the named adult framework treats it as additional evidence rather than one of the listed major markers.'),
 ('Frequent, fast NSVT on ambulatory monitoring','NSVT is clinically relevant and greater burden or faster/longer runs increase concern. In the named adult framework it informs additional risk assessment rather than being classified as one of the major-marker list.'),
 ('Left atrial anteroposterior diameter of fifty millimeters','LA size contributes to the HCM Risk-SCD model and can reflect disease burden. It is not a standalone major marker in the AHA/ACC adult list asked about here.')],
 [S('Use the Named Adult Framework','**One or more major adult HCM risk markers supports offering an ICD discussion under AHA/ACC guidance.** This is primary prevention, distinct from an ICD recommendation after documented arrest or sustained ventricular arrhythmia.',
    'Verify family circumstances, the nature of syncope and imaging measurements. A diagnostic feature such as mild wall thickening should not be promoted into a sudden-death marker without meeting the appropriate definition.',
    'A scarred apical aneurysm is an arrhythmic substrate even when global EF is preserved. Its relevance is not removed by the absence of resting LVOTO.'),
  S('Translate Risk Markers Into an Individual Decision','**Risk assessment requires shared discussion of arrhythmic risk, competing illness, device complications and patient preferences.** A marker supports an offer of protection; it does not predict that every untreated patient will experience an event.',
    'Extensive LGE and NSVT can influence decisions when the major-marker assessment is inconclusive. The adult framework must not be copied unchanged into children, where measurements and risk models differ.',
    'Repeat evaluation as the phenotype evolves and promptly investigate new syncope or sustained palpitations. A successful gradient treatment does not remove an independently relevant scar, prior event or family history. Device selection should consider pacing and ATP needs as well as the consequences of inappropriate shocks, lead problems and future replacements. Medical suppression of ventricular ectopy is not an equivalent substitute for an independently indicated ICD. Record which feature actually supports the discussion rather than labeling the patient high risk from the HCM diagnosis alone.')],['HCM2024'],style='direct',
 table=(['Major adult marker','Relevant definition'],[
 ['Familial HCM-related sudden death','First-degree or close relative aged ≤50 years; confirm attribution'],
 ['Massive LV hypertrophy','Maximal segment thickness ≥30 mm'],
 ['Recent likely arrhythmic syncope','Not explained by reflex mechanism or LVOTO'],
 ['LV apical aneurysm','With transmural scar or LGE'],
 ['LV systolic impairment','EF <50%']]))

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'HCM Risk Models and Additional CMR Evidence',
 'An adult with HCM has an estimated five-year HCM Risk-SCD value of 3%, no major AHA/ACC risk marker and LGE involving approximately 18% of LV mass. The patient asks whether the numerical estimate makes further ICD discussion unnecessary. Which response is most appropriate?',
 'Discuss the extensive scar as additional risk evidence; the low estimate does not end individualized ICD assessment',[
 ('Exclude ICD consideration because the model estimate is below four percent','A low model estimate informs assessment, but the model does not include every relevant scar or functional feature. Additional risk evidence can change the shared decision.'),
 ('Treat LGE above fifteen percent as a mandatory ICD indication','Extensive scar supports a risk discussion in this setting, but does not create a universal implantation mandate. Expected benefit, competing risk, measurement quality and patient preference remain part of the decision.'),
 ('Require inducible sustained VT at EPS before discussing an ICD','EPS can answer selected arrhythmia questions, but inducibility is not a prerequisite in the standard HCM primary-prevention framework. A negative result would not erase the supplied imaging evidence.')],
 [S('Know What the Model Includes and Omits','**HCM Risk-SCD integrates age, maximal wall thickness, LA diameter, maximal LVOT gradient, family history, NSVT and unexplained syncope.** It estimates a five-year outcome in an appropriate population rather than measuring risk directly.',
    'The conventional ESC risk bands are below 4%, 4 to below 6%, and at least 6%. They inform progressively stronger consideration of ICD therapy, while eligibility, clinical context and patient preference remain essential.',
    'Do not use an adult model unchanged in children or assume perfect validation after every intervention or in every HCM phenocopy. Check the model’s intended setting before interpreting its output.'),
  S('Interpret Scar as Additional Evidence','**Extensive LGE, commonly defined as at least 15% of LV mass, can support ICD consideration when conventional assessment is uncertain.** Measurement technique and the total phenotype affect interpretation.',
    'AHA/ACC distinguishes its major adult risk markers from additional findings such as extensive scar or NSVT. ESC combines model-based estimation with additional clinical evidence; these frameworks should be named rather than merged into an invented score.',
    'Explain the uncertainty of the absolute event estimate and the potential burdens of long-term device treatment. Scar burden is not a universal implantation mandate, but it should not be dismissed because an earlier calculation was low. Review rhythm monitoring and imaging quality, assess competing mortality and revisit the decision as new clinical features emerge. The defensible response is a renewed shared risk discussion, not automatic implantation from the scar number or automatic exclusion from the model number.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Anticoagulation for Clinical AF in HCM',
 'A 38-year-old man with established HCM has ECG-documented symptomatic paroxysmal AF. His CHA2DS2-VASc score is zero. There is no mechanical valve, significant mitral stenosis or anticoagulant contraindication. Which stroke-prevention strategy is appropriate?',
 'Oral anticoagulation, with an appropriate DOAC preferred',[
 ('No antithrombotic treatment until the conventional score reaches two','HCM-associated clinical AF has a disease-specific anticoagulation recommendation independent of the conventional score. Waiting for additional score components can leave a relevant embolic risk untreated.'),
 ('Aspirin as the initial alternative because the score is zero','Aspirin is not an equivalent substitute for indicated anticoagulation in HCM with clinical AF. A low conventional score does not create an aspirin-first pathway for this disease.'),
 ('Anticoagulation only during symptomatic episodes and the days immediately afterward','Intermittent symptoms do not reliably identify all AF exposure or thromboembolic risk. Long-term prevention is based on the established HCM-AF context, not an unvalidated symptom-triggered dosing schedule.')],
 [S('Use the HCM-Specific Embolic-Risk Recommendation','**Clinical AF in HCM warrants oral anticoagulation independent of CHA2DS2-VASc.** AHA/ACC guidance prefers DOACs when suitable, with vitamin K antagonists as the alternative.',
    'Choose the drug and dose using renal function, age, body weight, interactions and the relevant label. Mechanical valves and significant mitral stenosis create separate anticoagulant-selection rules; the stem excludes them.',
    'Assess bleeding risk and correct modifiable contributors, but do not confuse a bleeding score with a reason to substitute aspirin for effective stroke prevention.'),
  S('Distinguish Clinical AF From Short Device-Detected Episodes','**For subclinical AF lasting more than 24 hours in a single episode, the 2024 HCM guideline also recommends anticoagulation independent of the conventional score.** Shorter episodes need more individualized interpretation.',
    'For episodes longer than five minutes but shorter than twenty-four hours, anticoagulation can be beneficial after considering burden, underlying risk and bleeding. Do not apply the same certainty to a brief unverified device signal.',
    'Verify stored rhythm data when appropriate and address rate or rhythm control separately. Poorly tolerated AF can markedly impair filling in HCM and may justify cardioversion, antiarrhythmic therapy or ablation assessment. Restoration of sinus rhythm does not automatically remove the anticoagulation indication in a patient with established clinical AF. Review adherence and renal function and explain the difference between symptom control and embolic protection. The key distinction here is the disease-specific context: a score of zero does not overrule the supplied HCM-associated clinical AF.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Systolic Dysfunction in Established HCM',
 'A patient with established HCM develops persistent exertional limitation and LVEF 44%. Resting and provoked imaging show no significant LVOTO. Coronary assessment finds no explanatory disease. Which management interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'Treat the new systolic dysfunction and reassess advanced-HF and ICD needs using the HCM framework',[('Continue symptom treatment and reassess EF before initiating the systolic-HF pathway', 'In HCM, an EF below fifty percent signifies systolic dysfunction. Waiting for thirty-five percent misses an important disease-specific change and its risk implications.'), ('Add disopyramide to the symptom-control regimen', 'Disopyramide is used to reduce obstruction in suitable patients. Additional negative inotropy is not the appropriate response to nonobstructive systolic dysfunction.'), ('Refer for septal reduction to improve exertional dyspnea', 'Septal reduction can improve symptoms due to drug-refractory dynamic obstruction in suitable anatomy. The supplied nonobstructive phenotype with reduced EF instead requires assessment and treatment of systolic myocardial failure.')],
 [S('Recognize the Disease-Specific EF Threshold','**Persistent LVEF below 50% represents systolic dysfunction in HCM.** A patient whose earlier EF was hyperdynamic can have clinically important decline before reaching conventional severe-HFrEF thresholds.',
    'Reassess for coronary disease, tachyarrhythmia, valvular disease, drug effects and other contributors rather than assuming every deterioration is irreversible HCM progression.',
    'Repeat reliable imaging when a single unexpected value is uncertain and assess congestion, perfusion, rhythm and functional limitation. A disappearing gradient can reflect altered contractility rather than successful disease modification.'),
  S('Change the Treatment Objective','**Use guideline-directed systolic-HF treatment as appropriate and review negative inotropes.** The 2024 guidance calls for discontinuing cardiac myosin inhibitors when persistent systolic dysfunction develops.',
    'Verapamil, diltiazem and disopyramide can become unsuitable as the physiology changes. Continue individually indicated therapies after assessing pressure, conduction, renal function and tolerability rather than retaining an obstruction regimen by habit.',
    'EF below fifty is also a major adult HCM sudden-death risk marker, supporting an ICD discussion before simply importing the usual thirty-five-percent rule. Evaluate CRT when the electrical and clinical criteria support it, and refer for advanced-HF or transplant assessment when severe limitation persists despite appropriate care. Some HCM patients have advanced HF even with preserved EF because of restrictive filling or a small cavity; EF is not the sole referral criterion. Reassess rhythm and anticoagulation needs and explain why a treatment that helped earlier obstructive physiology may no longer fit the current presentation.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[1][0],'Genotype-Positive Relatives and Exercise Counseling',
 'A 16-year-old carries the family’s established pathogenic HCM variant. There are no symptoms, ECG abnormalities or imaging evidence of HCM. The family asks about follow-up and competitive sport. Which plan best fits AHA/ACC 2024 guidance?',
 'Continue serial phenotype surveillance with individualized sports assessment',[
 ('Repeat cardiac assessment only if symptoms develop','A pathogenic carrier can remain asymptomatic while the phenotype evolves. Scheduled adolescent surveillance remains appropriate; symptom-triggered review supplements rather than replaces it.'),
 ('Start preventive beta blockade and defer repeat imaging until adulthood','Beta blockade has symptom and arrhythmia roles in clinically expressed HCM, but is not established as a substitute for surveillance in a phenotype-negative carrier. The adolescent still needs periodic imaging and clinical review.'),
 ('Allow recreational activity but exclude competitive sport on genotype alone','Guidance allows genotype-positive, phenotype-negative individuals to participate in competitive sport. Assessment and counseling remain appropriate, but genotype alone does not create a universal exclusion rule.')],
 [S('Follow the Age-Dependent Phenotype','**A genotype-positive, phenotype-negative relative needs serial clinical assessment, ECG and cardiac imaging.** The 2024 US intervals are generally every one to two years in children and adolescents and every three to five years in adults.',
    'Assess sooner for syncope, exertional chest discomfort, sustained palpitations or a change in the family history. Routine intervals are not instructions to defer evaluation of new symptoms.',
    'Confirm that the familial result is pathogenic or likely pathogenic in an established disease gene. A VUS does not provide an equivalent basis for labeling a healthy person a confirmed carrier.'),
  S('Counsel Activity Without Treating Genotype as Disease Severity','**Competitive sport is reasonable for genotype-positive individuals without the HCM phenotype under the 2024 framework.** Discuss the person’s evaluation, sport demands and the plan for ongoing follow-up.',
    'Established HCM needs its own exercise and risk assessment. Mild-to-moderate recreational activity is generally beneficial; vigorous or competitive activity requires the applicable expert review and shared discussion rather than a blanket rule for every patient.',
    'An ICD is not a sports eligibility device and should follow an independent clinical indication. Discuss symptom reporting and practical emergency planning without promising zero risk or predicting inevitable disease. A reassuring current assessment supports activity decisions in the present, while surveillance addresses possible later expression. Changes in imaging, symptoms or arrhythmia can require revisiting participation. Separate transmission risk, penetrance and clinical severity during counseling so a molecular result does not become an unsupported prediction of the teenager’s future functional capacity.')],['HCM2024','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Etiological Assessment of Newly Diagnosed DCM',
 'A stable adult has newly recognized LV dilatation and LVEF 31%. Coronary and valve assessment does not explain the dysfunction. There is no shock, high-grade AV block or sustained ventricular arrhythmia. Baseline clinical and laboratory assessment has begun. Which additional investigation most broadly refines the myocardial phenotype before selecting more targeted tests?',
 'CMR with ventricular assessment and tissue characterization',[
 ('Broad serum viral antibody testing','Viral serology often reflects prior systemic exposure and does not reliably establish infection within myocardium. Targeted infection testing can be appropriate, but a broad antibody panel is not the general etiological solution.'),
 ('Endomyocardial biopsy','Biopsy can change urgent care in high-risk or specifically suspected inflammatory/infiltrative disease. This stable presentation does not make routine biopsy before a suitable CMR the preferred broad next step.'),
 ('Cardiac FDG-PET','FDG-PET is useful for selected inflammatory questions such as cardiac sarcoidosis when properly prepared. It is not the universal first myocardial characterization test for every stable unexplained DCM presentation.')],
 [S('Characterize the Phenotype and Search for a Cause','**DCM requires ventricular dilatation and systolic dysfunction not explained solely by abnormal loading conditions or coronary disease.** Once the phenotype is recognized, evaluate inherited and acquired contributors rather than treating idiopathic as the starting conclusion.',
    'CMR evaluates chamber function, nonischemic scar, regional abnormalities and clues to inflammation, iron or infiltration. Interpretation must integrate the clinical presentation; a pattern is not a standalone proof of a gene or pathogen.',
    'Obtain a detailed pedigree and review alcohol, cardiotoxic treatment, sustained tachycardia, pregnancy timing, endocrine/metabolic disease and extracardiac findings. Initial laboratory testing and rhythm assessment should be directed by the clinical context.'),
  S('Reserve Specialized Tests for a Defined Decision','**Biopsy is most useful when tissue diagnosis is expected to alter management.** Shock, important conduction disease, malignant arrhythmia, progressive dysfunction or a specific treatable histological suspicion can change the urgency and sequencing.',
    'FDG-PET answers selected questions about inflammatory activity and distribution; it requires preparation that suppresses physiological uptake. Broad viral antibody positivity does not establish a myocardial viral diagnosis or justify antiviral treatment.',
    'Consider genetic counseling and phenotype-directed testing when results can clarify cause, risk or family care, including presentations without an obvious positive pedigree. Begin appropriate HF treatment in parallel rather than postponing care until every etiological test returns. Reassess EF after therapy and investigate new arrhythmia or syncope promptly. This question asks for broad myocardial characterization in a stable patient, not a rule that CMR must precede biopsy in every fulminant or high-risk presentation.')],['CMP2023','MYO2025','GEN2022'])

R['RARE2026']=('EHRA/ACNAP rare primary arrhythmia syndromes consensus, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euag184')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'LMNA-Associated Cardiomyopathy',
 'Several relatives develop AV conduction disease and atrial arrhythmias in early adulthood, followed later by LV dysfunction. One requires pacing before appreciable chamber dilatation. Which gene is most strongly suggested by this familial sequence?',
 'LMNA',[
 ('TTN','Truncating TTN variants are an important DCM cause with variable penetrance and environmental interaction. Early prominent familial conduction disease preceding ventricular dysfunction is more characteristic of the supplied laminopathy sequence.'),
 ('DSP','DSP disease often features myocardial scar, ventricular arrhythmias and inflammatory chest-pain episodes, sometimes with substantial LV involvement. That phenotype differs from the dominant early conduction/atrial sequence supplied here.'),
 ('MYBPC3','MYBPC3 is a major sarcomeric HCM gene. Although phenotypes can evolve, a family dominated by early conduction disease followed by DCM more strongly suggests LMNA than a typical MYBPC3 presentation.')],
 [S('Recognize Electrical Disease Before Overt DCM','**LMNA encodes nuclear lamins A and C and can cause an inherited cardiomyopathy with early conduction and atrial electrical disease.** Ventricular dysfunction or dilatation can develop after these initial manifestations.',
    'The 2026 EHRA/ACNAP consensus explicitly recognizes that conduction abnormalities and early atrial arrhythmias can precede a manifest myocardial phenotype. A normal early echo should therefore not end the assessment in a strongly suggestive family.',
    'Some laminopathies have skeletal muscle or other extracardiac manifestations, while others appear predominantly cardiac. Their absence does not exclude LMNA-associated disease.'),
  S('Investigate the Family and the Full Arrhythmic Risk','**Use cardiovascular genetic counseling and a validated pathogenic or likely pathogenic result to guide cascade testing.** The phenotype suggests a testing priority but does not diagnose a specific variant from the ECG alone.',
    'Assess ventricular function, scar, atrial arrhythmia, AV conduction and ventricular arrhythmia longitudinally. A pacemaker corrects bradycardia but does not provide defibrillator protection against ventricular tachyarrhythmia.',
    'LMNA-associated risk can be important before EF falls below the conventional thirty-five-percent threshold. Use the gene-specific risk assessment and applicable guideline context, considering NSVT, AV conduction abnormalities, EF, sex and variant type rather than the gene name alone as an automatic device instruction. Review the possible need for defibrillation when planning pacing and discuss device complications and competing illness. Continue HF and AF management when indicated. Clinical family screening remains relevant if testing is negative or inconclusive, and a VUS must not be used as a validated predictive result. The diagnostic clue is the timing and clustering of electrical and myocardial disease across relatives, not the mere presence of one isolated AV block.')],['CMP2023','GEN2022','RARE2026'])

R['TTNALCOHOL']=('Genetic Etiology for Alcohol-Induced Cardiac Toxicity, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2018.03.462')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Titin Variants and Acquired Cardiomyopathy Triggers',
 'A patient with DCM and substantial previous alcohol exposure has an expertly classified pathogenic truncating TTN variant. Which interpretation best guides counseling?',
 'Inherited susceptibility and alcohol exposure can contribute together; address both the exposure and familial implications',[('Prioritize alcohol cessation and omit familial evaluation of this exposure-associated phenotype', 'An acquired exposure does not exclude inherited susceptibility. TTN truncating variants can contribute to vulnerability in alcohol-associated disease, so the result can still affect counseling and family evaluation.'), ('Counsel that the genetic result makes substantial reverse remodeling unlikely', 'Genetic susceptibility does not imply complete irreversibility. Removing a contributor and treating HF can improve function, although recovery and its durability vary.'), ('Use the proband’s age at presentation to predict the relatives’ age at onset', 'The pedigree helps estimate familial expression, but penetrance and severity vary with age, exposures and variant context. The proband’s age cannot reliably predict when or whether an individual relative will develop disease.')],
 [S('Integrate Genetic and Environmental Contributions','**A pathogenic TTN truncating variant can confer susceptibility that interacts with myocardial stressors.** A clinically important alcohol exposure and a genetic contribution need not be mutually exclusive explanations.',
    'The 2018 genetic alcohol-toxicity study supports a contribution from TTN truncating variants in alcohol-associated cardiomyopathy. It does not show that every person who drinks has a genetic cardiomyopathy or that every TTN finding is pathogenic.',
    'Interpret the actual variant through the laboratory classification, affected transcript and gene–disease evidence. Truncating findings in TTN are not uniformly equivalent, and an incidental or uncertain result requires specialist interpretation.'),
  S('Treat the Patient and Evaluate the Family','**Remove the harmful exposure and provide appropriate HF treatment while assessing familial risk.** Improvement after abstinence does not retrospectively prove that an established genetic contribution was absent.',
    'Offer genetic counseling and appropriate cascade evaluation after a convincing pathogenic or likely pathogenic result. Genotype-positive relatives can need surveillance even when the first ECG and imaging are normal.',
    'Base arrhythmia protection on clinical history, ventricular function, scar, rhythm findings and the applicable disease-specific framework rather than TTN carriage alone. Reassess function after treatment, and do not withdraw effective HF therapy solely because one later EF normalizes without considering relapse risk. Discuss nutrition, coexisting liver disease, adherence and other exposures as clinically relevant. The correct interpretation preserves two actionable conclusions: the exposure should be addressed and the inherited finding can matter for the family. It avoids both attributing all disease to lifestyle and treating genotype as an unmodifiable prediction of inevitable progression.')],['CMP2023','GEN2022','TTNALCOHOL'])

R['DSP2020']=('Desmoplakin cardiomyopathy: fibrotic and inflammatory phenotype study, 2020','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIRCULATIONAHA.119.044934')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Desmoplakin Cardiomyopathy and Myocarditis-Like Episodes',
 'A young adult has recurrent chest-pain episodes with troponin release and unobstructed coronary arteries. CMR shows LV-predominant nonischemic scar with relatively preserved EF. A sibling has a similar history and ventricular arrhythmia. Which inherited substrate particularly warrants investigation?',
 'DSP-associated cardiomyopathy',[
 ('LMNA-associated cardiomyopathy','LMNA disease can cause dangerous arrhythmia and myocardial dysfunction, often with early conduction and atrial disease. Recurrent inflammatory injury with LV scar is more characteristic of the supplied DSP pattern.'),
 ('MYBPC3-associated sarcomeric HCM','MYBPC3 is an important HCM gene, but the stem does not supply a hypertrophic phenotype. Familial myocarditis-like episodes and LV-predominant fibrosis suggest a different inherited substrate.'),
 ('GLA-associated Fabry cardiomyopathy','Fabry disease can cause LV hypertrophy and inferolateral scar, often with renal, neurological or other systemic clues. The recurrent familial injury pattern without the supplied storage phenotype more strongly suggests DSP disease.')],
 [S('Recognize an Inherited Substrate Behind Inflammatory Presentations','**DSP cardiomyopathy can cause episodic myocardial injury and LV fibrosis before marked systolic dysfunction.** Chest pain and troponin release may initially be labeled isolated myocarditis.',
    'Recurrent episodes, a suggestive pedigree, substantial nonischemic scar and ventricular arrhythmia increase concern for an inherited arrhythmogenic phenotype. A normal coronary angiogram excludes a particular ischemic explanation, not all important myocardial disease.',
    'Do not diagnose a specific variant from a scan. Use expert clinical evaluation, genetic counseling and validated molecular interpretation, while still investigating relevant acquired inflammatory causes.'),
  S('Assess Both Injury Activity and Durable Arrhythmic Risk','**The active inflammatory episode and the persistent scar are related but distinct management problems.** Tissue edema can change while a fibrotic arrhythmic substrate remains.',
    'Monitor rhythm, ventricular function and symptoms over time; preserved global EF does not imply absence of important ventricular risk. Consider the disease-specific risk context rather than relying only on conventional EF cut-offs.',
    'Provide appropriate family evaluation and individualized exercise advice. Treat HF and arrhythmia when present, and assess ICD need from the complete clinical picture rather than the DSP gene name alone. Immunomodulatory treatment for selected inflammatory presentations requires expert assessment and current evidence; the existence of a myocarditis-like episode does not establish that every carrier should receive chronic immunosuppression. Conversely, a genetic diagnosis does not make acute inflammation or hemodynamic deterioration unimportant. Explain why recurrent chest-pain episodes and a familial scar pattern merit a broader assessment than repeated reassurance after normal coronary studies.')],['CMP2023','MYO2025','DSP2020'])

R['ARVC2010']=('Revised ARVC Task Force diagnostic criteria, 2010','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehq025')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'CMR Structural Criteria for ARVC',
 'A man undergoing expert ARVC evaluation has reproducible regional RV dyskinesia, indexed RV end-diastolic volume 114 mL/m² and RVEF 43%. Under the revised 2010 Task Force CMR framework, how should this structural finding be counted?',
 'One major structural criterion; the regional abnormality and major volume threshold are present',[
 ('One minor structural criterion because EF is above forty percent','The major CMR criterion can use qualifying volume or EF in addition to the regional abnormality. EF above forty does not downgrade a volume that already reaches the male major threshold.'),
 ('Two major structural criteria because volume and regional motion are separately counted','The regional and quantitative findings form one structural criterion. They are not independent major categories that can be counted twice to establish a definite diagnosis.'),
 ('No structural criterion unless RVEF is at most forty percent','A qualifying indexed volume is an alternative to the major EF threshold. Requiring both volume and EF abnormalities incorrectly narrows the named framework.')],
 [S('Combine Regional and Quantitative Findings','**The 2010 CMR criterion requires a qualifying regional motion abnormality plus abnormal indexed volume or EF.** It is not established by an isolated large RV or a subjective motion impression alone.',
    'Major volume thresholds are RVEDVi at least 110 mL/m² in men and at least 100 mL/m² in women; RVEF at most 40% is the alternative major quantitative threshold.',
    'Minor volume bands are 100 to below 110 mL/m² in men and 90 to below 100 mL/m² in women; the alternative minor EF band is above 40% through 45%. Regional criteria still apply.'),
  S('Integrate the Finding Without Overdiagnosis','**A major imaging criterion is not the entire clinical diagnosis.** Integrate electrical, arrhythmic, tissue and familial evidence within one named framework, avoiding double counting within a category.',
    'In the 2010 framework, definite disease requires two major, one major plus two minor, or four minor criteria from different categories. Borderline disease uses one major plus one minor or three minor; possible disease uses one major or two minor from different categories. The combined CMR findings remain one structural-category contribution. Expert RV interpretation must also exclude athletic adaptation, pressure/volume overload and other mimics.',
    'The 2010 framework is useful for the specified RV phenotype, but it does not fully characterize every LV-predominant arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy. Contemporary assessment also considers scar distribution and genetics rather than declaring a patient disease-free whenever classic RV criteria are absent. A reassuring global EF cannot overrule a confirmed regional and volume abnormality, while an equivocal local contour should not be promoted into dyskinesia without careful review. Continue risk assessment and family evaluation after establishing the phenotype; the number of diagnostic criteria does not directly dictate a device prescription.')],['ARVC2010','CMP2023'],
 table=(['2010 CMR quantitative component','Major','Minor'],[
 ['Male RVEDVi','≥110 mL/m²','100 to <110 mL/m²'],
 ['Female RVEDVi','≥100 mL/m²','90 to <100 mL/m²'],
 ['RVEF alternative','≤40%','>40% to ≤45%']]))

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'ARVC ECG Criteria and Conduction Exclusions',
 'During an ARVC evaluation, a 24-year-old has T-wave inversion in V1–V3 and no complete RBBB. Under the revised 2010 Task Force framework, which interpretation is appropriate?',
 'One major repolarization criterion',[
 ('One minor repolarization criterion','Adult V1–V2 inversion without complete RBBB can be a minor finding, while the supplied extension through V3 meets the major rule. The separate complete-RBBB context uses different lead requirements.'),
 ('One major depolarization criterion','T-wave inversion concerns repolarization. A reproducible epsilon wave was a major delayed-activation finding in the 2010 framework, but no such post-QRS signal is supplied here.'),
 ('One minor depolarization criterion','Terminal activation delay and selected signal-averaged ECG abnormalities are minor delayed-activation findings. They require their own measurements and cannot be inferred from the supplied T-wave inversion.')],
 [S('Check Age, Leads and Bundle-Branch Context','**Right-precordial T-wave inversion must be interpreted with age and conduction pattern.** In the named 2010 framework, V1–V3 inversion or extension beyond those leads in someone older than fourteen without complete RBBB is a major repolarization finding.',
    'When complete RBBB is present, inversion through V4 is a minor criterion in that framework. Do not apply the non-RBBB rule to expected secondary repolarization changes without checking the QRS.',
    'Juvenile and athletic patterns, lead placement and other right-heart or inflammatory disease can complicate interpretation. A criterion is not proof of a unique etiology.'),
  S('Keep Electrical Categories Distinct','**Depolarization findings concern delayed ventricular activation, whereas T-wave findings concern repolarization.** Confirm reproducibility and technical quality before calling a small post-QRS deflection an epsilon wave.',
    'A terminal activation duration of at least 55 ms from the S-wave nadir to QRS end in V1, V2 or V3, without complete RBBB, is a minor delayed-activation criterion under the named framework.',
    'Integrate the tracing with ventricular imaging, arrhythmia morphology, symptoms and the pedigree. Contemporary interpretation recognizes limitations in reproducibility of some older electrical findings and the broader spectrum of arrhythmogenic disease. Do not diagnose ARVC solely because an automated ECG report mentions right-precordial inversion, and do not exclude it because an epsilon wave is absent. New exertional syncope or sustained ventricular arrhythmia demands urgent assessment independent of whether every classification item has been collected. Risk treatment follows the patient’s actual phenotype and clinical history rather than one isolated ECG label.')],['ARVC2010','CMP2023'],style='direct')

R['SARCOID2024']=('AHA cardiac sarcoidosis scientific statement, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001240')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Inflammatory Mimics of Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathy',
 'A patient with ventricular arrhythmia and RV abnormalities is referred with a presumed inherited ARVC diagnosis. Further assessment finds high-grade AV block, patchy basal septal LV enhancement and metabolically active hilar lymph nodes. Which alternative particularly warrants directed investigation?',
 'Cardiac sarcoidosis with possible extracardiac involvement',[
 ('Giant-cell myocarditis','Giant-cell disease can cause block, ventricular arrhythmia and rapidly progressive dysfunction, so it remains an important tissue differential. The nodal findings and basal septal phenotype particularly favor directed sarcoid investigation rather than assuming isolated giant-cell myocardial disease.'),
 ('Isolated lymphocytic myocarditis','Lymphocytic myocarditis can produce nonischemic injury and ventricular arrhythmia. It does not as readily unify metabolically active hilar nodes with the supplied conduction and septal findings; tissue and systemic assessment help distinguish these processes.'),
 ('DSP-associated inflammatory cardiomyopathy','DSP disease can cause recurrent injury, LV scar and ventricular arrhythmia and may mimic acquired myocarditis. The supplied extracardiac nodal activity and high-grade block instead strengthen the sarcoid pathway, while genetic assessment remains appropriate when other evidence supports it.')],
 [S('Reopen the Etiological Diagnosis When Findings Do Not Fit','**Cardiac sarcoidosis can mimic arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy.** Conduction disease, ventricular arrhythmia, regional dysfunction and nonischemic scar can overlap with inherited phenotypes.',
    'Basal septal disease and extracardiac findings provide useful clues, but neither CMR enhancement nor FDG uptake alone proves sarcoidosis. Inadequate preparation can also produce misleading myocardial uptake.',
    'The differential of an abnormal RV includes myocarditis, infarction, pulmonary hypertension, congenital volume overload and other cardiomyopathies. A negative family history does not by itself establish an acquired cause.'),
  S('Choose Tests That Clarify Both Cause and Treatment','**Integrate CMR, appropriately prepared FDG-PET and tissue assessment where informative.** An accessible abnormal extracardiac site can provide a safer diagnostic target than unguided myocardial sampling.',
    'A negative endomyocardial biopsy does not reliably exclude patchy disease. Imaging- or electroanatomically directed sampling may improve yield in selected cases, and interpretation should follow a named clinical framework.',
    'Assess inflammatory activity and arrhythmic/conduction risk separately. Immunosuppression may address active inflammation, but scar and device indications can persist despite response. High-grade block requires immediate clinical management rather than waiting for the entire etiological work-up. Conversely, a pacemaker prescription does not settle whether defibrillation capability is also needed. Genetic assessment remains appropriate when the pedigree or phenotype suggests inherited disease; coexistence and uncertainty require careful review rather than replacing one premature label with another. The supplied nodal and septal findings justify a directed inflammatory evaluation before treating the RV appearance as a proven familial diagnosis.')],['CMP2023','MYO2025','SARCOID2024'])

R['ATHLETE2024']=('HRS athlete arrhythmia evaluation and return-to-play consensus, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.hrthm.2024.05.018')
add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Exercise Counseling in Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathy',
 'A person with definite PKP2-associated ARVC wishes to continue competitive endurance racing. Symptoms are controlled and an ICD has been implanted for an independent indication. Which counseling statement is most appropriate?',
 'Develop a lower-intensity activity plan that avoids high-intensity endurance exposure',[
 ('Continue racing provided stored ICD data show no recent therapies','The absence of therapies is reassuring but does not establish safety of the training exposure. An ICD does not prevent exercise-associated progression, and its counters are not a validated clearance test.'),
 ('Stop competition but retain equally intense solo endurance training','Competitive status is not the only determinant of biological stress. High-intensity endurance exposure can still accelerate disease and arrhythmia when the same workload is performed alone.'),
 ('Restrict endurance training only during periods of symptomatic palpitations','Symptoms do not reliably track substrate progression or all arrhythmias. A disease-modifying exercise recommendation should not depend solely on whether the patient feels palpitations during a particular week.')],
 [S('Recognize Exercise as a Disease Modifier','**High-intensity exercise can increase arrhythmic risk and accelerate ARVC expression.** The concern is not limited to symptoms during a particular workout.',
    'Evidence is especially relevant to desmosomal disease such as PKP2-associated ARVC. Even genotype-positive, phenotype-negative individuals need genotype-informed discussion of intensive endurance exposure rather than automatic reassurance from normal initial imaging.',
    'This differs from applying a single exercise rule to every cardiomyopathy or every genetic result. A VUS is not equivalent to an established pathogenic variant, and HCM participation guidance must not be transferred unchanged to ARVC.'),
  S('Develop a Practical Individualized Activity Plan','**An ICD treats episodes rather than removing their trigger or preventing myocardial remodeling.** Exercise counseling remains necessary after implantation, ablation or temporary drug suppression.',
    'Discuss lower-intensity alternatives, cumulative exposure, symptoms, ventricular function and rhythm findings with an experienced service. Avoid translating a recommendation against intense endurance racing into a blanket prohibition of ordinary movement.',
    'Arrange follow-up and reassess if syncope, sustained palpitations, declining function or new device therapies occur. Explain that absence of shocks may reflect device programming, successful treatment or a quiet interval, not proof that the underlying disease has resolved. Include emergency planning and the patient’s goals, recognizing the effect of restrictions on wellbeing and adherence. A return-to-play decision should account for the specific phenotype and the evidence on exercise-related progression rather than relying solely on a defibrillator or a normal resting pulse. The aim is a feasible durable reduction in harmful exposure with preservation of appropriate activity, not a promise of zero residual risk.')],['CMP2023','ATHLETE2024'])

add(SECTIONS[2][0],'Arrhythmogenic Cardiomyopathy: Treating More Than the EF',
 'A patient with established arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy has LVEF 48%, documented sustained VT and progressive RV dysfunction. No reversible trigger explains the episode. Which management principle is most appropriate?',
 'Use sustained-VT and biventricular findings to plan ICD and HF care',[('Optimize HF therapy and revisit ICD candidacy if LV EF falls to thirty-five percent', 'The conventional primary-prevention EF threshold is not a prerequisite after every sustained ventricular event or in every inherited arrhythmogenic substrate. The clinical episode and disease-specific risk require their own assessment.'), ('Choose catheter ablation as the initial replacement for secondary-prevention device assessment', 'Ablation can reduce episodes but does not cure a progressive myocardial disorder. Function, arrhythmia, family implications and independent protection needs remain relevant after procedural success.'), ('Use LV-directed HF therapy and defer advanced-HF review until LV function worsens', 'LV-directed treatment is important when LV dysfunction is present, but it does not address all consequences of progressive RV failure. Severe RV dysfunction and arrhythmia can justify advanced-HF review while LV EF remains relatively preserved.')],
 [S('Integrate Electrical and Mechanical Disease','**Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy can produce clinically consequential ventricular arrhythmia before severe global LV impairment.** Assess the recorded event, hemodynamic tolerance, scar, ventricular function and reversible contributors.',
    'Evaluate both ventricles and review the phenotype and gene-specific context where established. A conventional LVEF threshold cannot summarize every arrhythmic or HF risk in this disease spectrum.',
    'Clarify whether the event creates a secondary-prevention indication and consider expected functional survival, competing illness and patient preferences. A decision about protection is distinct from a decision about suppressing recurrent episodes.'),
  S('Use Complementary Therapies for Complementary Problems','**HF treatment, activity modification, antiarrhythmics, ablation and device protection address different components.** No single intervention should be described as a universal cure for inherited myocardial disease.',
    'Treat congestion and ventricular dysfunction according to the clinical phenotype, with renal, pressure and electrolyte monitoring. Severe RV disease can require careful volume management and advanced-HF assessment.',
    'Ablation can reduce symptomatic VT and shock burden, sometimes requiring approaches tailored to epicardial or intramural substrate. Antiarrhythmics have disease- and organ-specific harms and do not automatically replace an indicated ICD. Continue familial counseling and appropriate relative assessment after identifying a convincing genetic cause. Reassess the prevention plan as function, arrhythmia and goals change. The clinically useful interpretation is to preserve attention to the serious documented rhythm and RV progression rather than waiting for the LV EF to cross a threshold designed for another primary-prevention decision.')],['CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Restrictive Cardiomyopathy Versus Pericardial Constriction',
 'A patient has raised filling pressures and a restrictive transmitral pattern. The team is distinguishing myocardial restriction from constrictive pericarditis. Which invasive finding most specifically supports constriction rather than myocardial restriction?',
 'Reciprocal respiratory changes in simultaneous RV and LV systolic pressures',[
 ('A dip-and-plateau contour in ventricular diastolic pressure','A square-root contour can occur with either restricted myocardial filling or pericardial constriction. It supports abnormal diastolic physiology but does not independently establish the pericardial mechanism.'),
 ('Similar right- and left-sided end-diastolic pressures','Approximate diastolic pressure equalization can occur in constriction but overlaps with other conditions. It is less discriminating than properly demonstrated respiratory ventricular discordance.'),
 ('Elevated right atrial pressure with a prominent y descent','A rapid early filling phase followed by limitation can produce a prominent y descent in either disorder. The finding alone does not localize the restriction to the pericardium.')],
 [S('Identify the Location of the Filling Constraint','**Restrictive cardiomyopathy reflects abnormal myocardial compliance; constriction reflects an external pericardial constraint.** Both can produce congestion, impaired filling and relatively preserved global EF.',
    'Constrictive physiology produces exaggerated ventricular interdependence and altered transmission of intrathoracic pressure. During inspiration, RV and LV systolic pressures can change in opposite directions on simultaneous recordings.',
    'Myocardial restriction generally preserves concordant respiratory changes. Interpret recordings with appropriate acquisition and clinical context; a single nonsimultaneous pressure trace cannot establish ventricular discordance.'),
  S('Integrate Hemodynamics With Imaging and Etiology','**Diastolic pressure equalization and a dip-and-plateau contour are not unique to constriction.** They should not replace an assessment of respiratory physiology.',
    'Echo can assess septal shift, tissue Doppler and hepatic venous flow; CMR or CT can assess pericardial structure and myocardial disease. A normal pericardial thickness does not by itself exclude constrictive physiology.',
    'Myocardial restriction can arise from amyloid, storage disease, endomyocardial disease or inherited abnormalities. Biatrial enlargement and a nondilated ventricular appearance suggest a phenotype, not a unique cause. Mixed myocardial and pericardial disease, prior radiation, loading conditions and technical limitations can complicate the distinction. The diagnosis matters because treating a reversible or surgically remediable pericardial problem differs from treating an infiltrative myocardial disease. Use multimodality assessment when noninvasive findings conflict, and investigate the underlying etiology rather than stopping at the word restrictive.')],['CMP2023','MYO2025'])

R['AMYLOID2023']=('ACC cardiac amyloidosis expert consensus decision pathway, 2023','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2022.11.022')
R['ATTR2025']=('ACC transthyretin cardiac amyloidosis concise clinical guidance, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2025.09.004')
R['ASH2026']=('ASH light-chain amyloidosis diagnostic guideline, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1182/bloodadvances.2025017073')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'The Complete Monoclonal Screen in Suspected Cardiac Amyloid',
 'A patient has otherwise unexplained increased LV wall thickness, restrictive filling and clinical red flags for cardiac amyloidosis. Which laboratory set is required to assess a monoclonal light-chain process before applying the nonbiopsy ATTR pathway?',
 'Serum free light chains plus serum and urine immunofixation',[
 ('Serum and urine protein electrophoresis without immunofixation','Electrophoresis without immunofixation can miss a small monoclonal component. It is not an adequate substitute for the more sensitive complete screen used in the diagnostic pathway.'),
 ('Serum free light chains plus serum immunofixation alone','These are important components, but omission of urine immunofixation makes the proposed screen incomplete. A reassuring partial result should not be used to claim that the entire light-chain assessment is negative.'),
 ('Serum and urine immunofixation without a free-light-chain assay','Immunofixation identifies monoclonal proteins, but the free-light-chain assay adds complementary information. Omitting it does not complete the recommended three-part screen.')],
 [S('Exclude the Urgent Alternative Before Assigning ATTR','**The recommended monoclonal screen combines serum free light chains, serum immunofixation and urine immunofixation.** This is a coordinated investigation, not a choice among interchangeable single tests.',
    'AL amyloidosis can cause cardiac disease despite a small plasma-cell clone. A routine serum electrophoresis report without an obvious spike does not reliably exclude that process.',
    'Interpret free-light-chain concentrations and ratio with renal function and the laboratory method. CKD can alter results without proving a pathogenic clone; abnormal results still require appropriate expert interpretation.'),
  S('Understand What Positive and Negative Results Mean','**A monoclonal protein is a reason for directed tissue and hematologic evaluation, not proof that the cardiac deposits are AL.** Age-related monoclonal gammopathy can coexist with ATTR.',
    'A negative complete screen permits consideration of the validated nonbiopsy ATTR pathway when imaging and scintigraphy requirements are also met. It does not make any unexplained wall thickening ATTR by itself.',
    'The 2026 ASH diagnostic guideline emphasizes timely biopsy with validated subtyping when AL is suspected. Bone scintigraphy is not an AL diagnostic substitute. Echo findings such as relative apical strain preservation and CMR tissue patterns raise suspicion but do not reliably identify the fibril protein. Arrange prompt specialist evaluation when the monoclonal screen and cardiac findings are concerning rather than waiting for progressive HF. The clinical purpose of the complete screen is to choose a safe diagnostic branch and avoid mistaking a treatable, time-sensitive plasma-cell disorder for a transthyretin condition.')],['AMYLOID2023','ATTR2025','ASH2026'])

R['AMYLOIDESC']=('ESC cardiac amyloidosis diagnosis and treatment position statement, 2021','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehab072')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Nonbiopsy Diagnosis of ATTR Cardiac Amyloidosis',
 'A patient has a typical cardiac amyloid phenotype. Serum free-light-chain assessment and serum/urine immunofixation are reassuring, without evidence of a clonal process. Bone-tracer scintigraphy shows grade 2 uptake, and SPECT confirms uptake in myocardium rather than blood pool. Which next interpretation is appropriate?',
 'Diagnose ATTR noninvasively and arrange TTR sequencing',[
 ('Obtain endomyocardial biopsy to establish the fibril type before proceeding','Typed biopsy is appropriate with a monoclonal protein, equivocal uptake or another discordant finding. Here the compatible phenotype, confirmed grade 2 myocardial uptake and negative complete clonal screen meet the validated nonbiopsy ATTR criteria.'),
 ('Obtain CMR tissue characterization to establish the fibril type before proceeding','CMR is useful when involvement or an alternative myocardial disorder remains uncertain. It does not reliably type the precursor protein, whereas the supplied complete scintigraphy and laboratory combination already supports ATTR.'),
 ('Repeat scintigraphy after an interval to establish diagnostic uptake progression','Repeat imaging can help resolve a technically inadequate or equivocal examination. This study already confirms grade 2 myocardial uptake by SPECT; progression is not a requirement of the validated diagnostic pathway.')],
 [S('Require Every Component of the Validated Pathway','**A compatible cardiac phenotype, grade 2 or 3 myocardial bone-tracer uptake and absence of a clonal light-chain process can establish ATTR-CM without biopsy.** Appropriate tracers include PYP, DPD or HMDP.',
    'Tomographic confirmation matters: residual blood pool and overlapping structures can imitate cardiac uptake on planar views. Do not infer true myocardial uptake from a planar appearance or a numerical ratio alone.',
    'Grade 1 uptake does not meet this nonbiopsy rule. Negative or equivocal scintigraphy also needs interpretation when clinical suspicion remains high; it does not universally exclude all amyloid types or every ATTR variant.'),
  S('Do Not Transfer the Rule to a Different Diagnostic Branch','**The monoclonal assessment must include serum free light chains and serum and urine immunofixation.** A positive clonal screen removes the certainty of the simple scan-based ATTR diagnosis and usually requires typed tissue.',
    'Bone-tracer uptake can occur in AL, and a monoclonal gammopathy can coexist with ATTR. Neither the scan nor the blood result should independently decide the fibril type when they conflict.',
    'After ATTR is established, arrange genetic counseling and TTR sequencing even in an older patient without a known affected relative. Evaluate neuropathy, conduction disease, AF, congestion and the suitability of current disease-modifying treatments. A nonbiopsy diagnosis is not a shortcut around the whole evaluation; it is a specifically validated combination of mutually supporting results. Preserve the actual laboratory and SPECT findings in the record so future clinicians can distinguish this pathway from an incompletely investigated positive scan.')],['AMYLOIDESC','AMYLOID2023','ATTR2025'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Positive Scintigraphy With a Monoclonal Protein',
 'A patient with a convincing cardiac amyloid phenotype has grade 3 myocardial PYP uptake confirmed by SPECT. Serum immunofixation also identifies a monoclonal protein. Which next diagnostic strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Obtain appropriate tissue confirmation and validated amyloid typing, with prompt hematologic assessment',[
 ('Diagnose ATTR from the high uptake grade and treat the monoclonal protein as incidental','Monoclonal gammopathy can be incidental, but that cannot be assumed. AL may show bone-tracer uptake, so a high grade does not rescue the nonbiopsy ATTR rule when the clonal screen is positive.'),
 ('Diagnose AL solely from the monoclonal protein and start clone-directed therapy without typing','A monoclonal protein does not prove the identity of the deposits. ATTR and a coincidental clone can coexist, making tissue confirmation and protein typing necessary before attributing disease to AL.'),
 ('Repeat PYP after a short interval to determine the deposited protein from uptake change','Changes in tracer uptake do not provide a validated substitute for typing in this discordant branch. Repeating the scan can delay a time-sensitive AL evaluation without resolving the causal question.')],
 [S('A Positive Clone Changes the Diagnostic Branch','**The nonbiopsy ATTR rule requires exclusion of a clonal light-chain process.** When a monoclonal protein is present, both AL and ATTR with coincidental gammopathy remain possible.',
    'Congo-red-positive tissue demonstrates amyloid but does not by itself identify the fibril precursor. Use a validated typing method, commonly mass-spectrometry-based proteomics in an expert laboratory.',
    'Choose the tissue strategy according to organ involvement, urgency and local expertise. ASH 2026 permits an initial combined fat-pad and marrow approach or endomyocardial biopsy in the appropriate suspected cardiac-AL setting.'),
  S('Do Not Use a Negative Surrogate Sample to End the Investigation','**Fat-pad or marrow sampling can be negative despite clinically important cardiac disease.** If suspicion persists and surrogate tissue is uninformative, evaluate biopsy of the affected organ or other suitable material.',
    'Endomyocardial biopsy can directly establish cardiac deposition and enable typing, but requires experienced acquisition and pathology interpretation. Tissue choice should answer the actual diagnostic uncertainty rather than simply favoring the least invasive test regardless of yield.',
    'Arrange hematology and cardiology assessment promptly because untreated cardiac AL can progress rapidly. Do not expose a patient with ATTR and incidental gammopathy to an unsupported plasma-cell regimen, and do not delay AL care by assuming every strongly positive PYP scan is transthyretin disease. After typing, assess disease extent and select subtype-specific therapy. Explain why two positive tests can create a need for a third definitive assessment: they measure different biological processes and do not establish that the circulating clone produced the myocardial deposits.')],['AMYLOID2023','ATTR2025','ASH2026'])

add(SECTIONS[3][0],'TTR Sequencing After Establishing Cardiac ATTR',
 'An 81-year-old with confirmed ATTR cardiac amyloidosis has no recognized neuropathy and no known affected relatives. Which next approach appropriately establishes the genetic subtype?',
 'Offer genetic counseling and TTR sequencing',[('Classify the subtype from age at presentation and the pedigree', 'Older age and a negative pedigree make wild-type ATTR more likely but do not determine the genotype. Late penetrance and unrecognized affected relatives can conceal a pathogenic TTR variant.'), ('Classify the subtype from a formal neurological assessment', 'A neurological assessment characterizes systemic involvement and guides care. Some pathogenic TTR variants produce predominantly cardiac disease, so absence of neuropathy cannot establish wild-type ATTR.'), ('Obtain a nerve-conduction study before deciding whether to offer TTR sequencing', 'Electrophysiology can characterize suspected peripheral neuropathy, but cardiac-predominant variant ATTR may occur without it. Neuropathy documentation is not a prerequisite for sequencing an established ATTR proband.')],
 [S('Separate Protein Identification From Genetic Subtyping','**ATTR identifies transthyretin as the fibril precursor; it does not by itself distinguish variant from wild-type TTR.** Neither a positive bone scan nor transthyretin typing provides the DNA result.',
    'Offer TTR sequencing with counseling after ATTR is established. A pathogenic variant can explain cardiac-predominant disease even when the presentation is late and the family history appears negative.',
    'Review neuropathic, autonomic and other organ manifestations rather than assuming the initial cardiac presentation describes the entire phenotype. Variant expression can differ among relatives.'),
  S('Use the Result to Guide Family Care','**A convincing pathogenic or likely pathogenic variant enables targeted cascade evaluation.** Counsel on autosomal dominant transmission, age-dependent penetrance and the limits of predicting severity from a genotype alone.',
    'A variant of uncertain significance is not equivalent to a validated familial cause. If sequencing supports wild-type disease, do not prescribe predictive family testing as though the patient has a demonstrated inherited variant.',
    'Treatment selection also depends on cardiac stage, associated neuropathy, patient goals, access and current evidence; genotype is only one component. Do not withhold subtype assessment because disease-modifying options exist for more than one ATTR form. Document the actual genetic conclusion rather than using senile or familial as a substitute for testing. A molecular diagnosis can be clinically informative for the older patient and substantially change counseling for younger relatives, while avoiding unsupported predictions that every carrier will develop the same organ involvement.')],['AMYLOID2023','ATTR2025','GEN2022'])

R['VUTLABEL']=('Vutrisiran US prescribing information, 2025 ATTR-CM indication','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/215515s006lbl.pdf')
R['ACORLABEL']=('Acoramidis current US prescribing information','https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=913552ef-875d-4cb7-bf05-a7d20a394c38')
R['TAFALABEL']=('Tafamidis US prescribing information','https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/lookup.cfm?setid=1b4121ee-a733-4456-a917-be2603477839')
R['ATTRACT']=('ATTR-ACT randomized tafamidis trial, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa1805689')
R['ATTRIBUTE']=('ATTRibute-CM randomized acoramidis trial, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2305434')
R['HELIOSB']=('HELIOS-B randomized vutrisiran trial, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2409134')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'ATTR Treatment: Silencing Versus Tetramer Stabilization',
 'An adult with confirmed ATTR cardiomyopathy discusses disease-modifying therapy. Which option reduces hepatic transthyretin production through RNA interference rather than primarily stabilizing the circulating tetramer?',
 'Vutrisiran',[
 ('Tafamidis','Tafamidis binds and stabilizes transthyretin, reducing tetramer dissociation. It is an established ATTR-CM treatment, but does not act through RNA-mediated suppression of hepatic production.'),
 ('Acoramidis','Acoramidis is another transthyretin stabilizer with an ATTR-CM indication. Its mechanism differs from the small-interfering-RNA approach asked about here.'),
 ('Diflunisal','Diflunisal can stabilize transthyretin and has been used off label in selected amyloid settings. It is not an RNA-interference therapy, and NSAID-related renal, bleeding and fluid-retention concerns limit suitability in cardiac disease.')],
 [S('Choose a Disease-Modifying Strategy After Confirming the Subtype','**Vutrisiran is a transthyretin-directed small interfering RNA that reduces hepatic TTR synthesis.** The 2025 US indication includes adult wild-type and hereditary ATTR cardiomyopathy, rather than restricting its use to hereditary neuropathy.',
    'Tafamidis and acoramidis stabilize the tetramer and reduce the tendency toward dissociation and amyloid formation. These mechanisms differ from directly removing an existing cardiac deposit or treating a plasma-cell clone.',
    'The treatment discussion must follow adequate typing. Neither a stabilizer nor a TTR silencer is the appropriate disease-specific substitute for clone-directed treatment of AL amyloidosis.'),
  S('Compare Regimens and Follow the Clinical Response','**Current labeled regimens differ substantially between agents.** Do not treat tablet strengths, salt formulations or injection intervals as interchangeable.',
    'Vutrisiran reduces circulating vitamin A; supplement at the recommended daily allowance rather than using high doses to normalize the serum result, and investigate ocular symptoms appropriately. Review the complete label and the patient’s clinical context.',
    'Assess cardiac stage, neuropathy, expected benefit, access, adverse effects and the patient’s preferences. The 2025 ACC guidance incorporates more than one evidence-supported disease-modifying approach; a newer option does not establish head-to-head superiority or a universal combination requirement. Continue careful congestion treatment, rhythm and conduction assessment and indicated embolic prevention. Symptom change, functional status, HF events and biomarkers complement imaging during follow-up. Do not label persistent wall thickness after treatment as proof of failure or assume that biochemical TTR lowering alone guarantees individual clinical recovery. Trial populations, background treatment and outcomes must be compared carefully before ranking drugs across separate studies.'),
  S('Interpret the Landmark Trials Within Their Designs','**These placebo-controlled trials establish benefit for different disease-modifying approaches, not a direct ranking of the drugs.** Their endpoints and background treatment differ.',
    'ATTR-ACT (2018) followed 441 patients for 30 months. Pooled tafamidis reduced mortality from 42.9% to 29.5% (HR 0.70, 95% CI 0.51–0.96), with fewer cardiovascular hospitalizations. Its primary hierarchy combined mortality and cardiovascular-hospitalization frequency.',
    'ATTRibute-CM (2024) used a 30-month four-level hierarchy: death, cardiovascular hospitalization, NT-proBNP change and six-minute walk change. The analysis favored acoramidis over placebo with a win ratio of 1.8 (95% CI 1.4–2.2); a win ratio is not a mortality hazard ratio.',
    'HELIOS-B (2025 publication) randomized 654 patients, including participants already receiving tafamidis. Vutrisiran reduced all-cause death and recurrent cardiovascular events over up to 36 months (overall HR 0.72, 95% CI 0.56–0.93). The separate population not taking tafamidis at baseline also benefited. This design does not randomize every possible combination against each active monotherapy.')],['ATTR2025','VUTLABEL','ACORLABEL','TAFALABEL','ATTRACT','ATTRIBUTE','HELIOSB'],style='direct',
 table=(['Agent','Usual adult US-labeled ATTR-CM regimen'],[
 ['Tafamidis free acid','61 mg orally once daily'],
 ['Tafamidis meglumine','80 mg orally once daily; not mg-for-mg interchangeable with free acid'],
 ['Acoramidis','712 mg orally twice daily'],
 ['Vutrisiran','25 mg subcutaneously every 3 months']]))

R['ANDROMEDA']=('ANDROMEDA randomized trial, 2021','https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2028631')
R['ANDROMEDA2026']=('ANDROMEDA final survival analysis, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1182/blood.2025032099')
R['DARALABEL']=('Daratumumab/hyaluronidase US prescribing information, 2025','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/761145s032lbl.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Clone-Directed Treatment of Cardiac AL Amyloidosis',
 'Biopsy and validated typing establish systemic AL amyloidosis with cardiac involvement. A treatment-naive patient has Mayo cardiac stage II disease, stable BP, adequate performance status and no major contraindication to the proposed drugs. Which initial clone-directed regimen has the strongest randomized support?',
 'Daratumumab plus bortezomib, cyclophosphamide and dexamethasone',[
 ('Bortezomib, cyclophosphamide and dexamethasone without daratumumab','VCd is an active regimen and was the randomized comparator. In an eligible patient without the supplied contraindications, adding daratumumab produces deeper responses and has supportive final survival evidence.'),
 ('Daratumumab monotherapy','Daratumumab alone has activity, particularly in previously treated AL or selected patients unable to tolerate combinations. ANDROMEDA tested daratumumab with VCd in newly diagnosed eligible patients, so monotherapy does not reproduce the strongest randomized initial-treatment evidence here.'),
 ('Oral melphalan and dexamethasone','Melphalan-dexamethasone is an active historical regimen with selected uses when contemporary combinations are unsuitable or unavailable. It does not displace the randomized daratumumab-VCd strategy in this treatment-naive patient able to receive the proposed agents.')],
 [S('Suppress the Pathogenic Light-Chain Source Promptly','**AL treatment targets the plasma-cell clone producing amyloidogenic light chains.** Rapid hematologic control matters because ongoing light-chain toxicity can worsen organ dysfunction before a large tissue burden is apparent.',
    'Confirm fibril typing rather than treating a monoclonal protein alone. Stage cardiac and renal involvement, assess autonomic dysfunction and define the patient’s tolerance before starting a regimen.',
    'Coordinate hematology and cardiology care. Manage congestion carefully, monitor rhythm and pressure and anticipate that hematologic improvement may precede a measurable organ response.'),
  S('Use Trial Evidence Without Extending It to Excluded Patients','**ANDROMEDA established daratumumab-VCd against VCd in newly diagnosed AL.** The 2026 final analysis, at median 61.4 months, reported an overall-survival HR of 0.62, 95% CI 0.42–0.90, favoring the daratumumab strategy.',
    'The trial excluded Mayo cardiac stage IIIB. The US label cautions against extrapolation to highly advanced cardiac AL outside controlled trials; such patients need an individualized expert plan rather than an automatic full-intensity prescription.',
    'Review neuropathy, infection risk, cytopenias, renal function, steroid-associated fluid retention and treatment reactions. Daratumumab can interfere with blood-bank testing, so obtain the necessary pretreatment compatibility information. Premedication, antiviral and other supportive measures follow the regimen and patient’s risks. Dose modification can be essential in fragile patients; the trial schedule is a reference regimen rather than a command to ignore organ dysfunction. Reassess light-chain response and cardiac status during treatment, and consider transplant eligibility through specialist review rather than assuming all cardiac involvement prohibits transplantation.')],['ANDROMEDA','ANDROMEDA2026','DARALABEL','AMYLOID2023'],
 table=(['ANDROMEDA reference schedule','Regimen detail'],[
 ['Bortezomib','1.3 mg/m² SC weekly'],
 ['Cyclophosphamide','300 mg/m² weekly; maximum 500 mg'],
 ['Dexamethasone','40 mg weekly; 20 mg allowed by clinical judgment'],
 ['VCd duration','Six 28-day cycles'],
 ['Daratumumab SC formulation','1800 mg with hyaluronidase: weekly in cycles 1–2, every 2 weeks in cycles 3–6, then every 4 weeks; up to 24 total cycles']]))

R['FABRYGENE']=('Fabry disease, GeneReviews','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK1292/')
R['AGALSIDASE']=('Agalsidase beta US prescribing information','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/103979s5313lbl.pdf')
R['MIGALASTAT']=('Migalastat US prescribing information, 2025','https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/208623s009lbl.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Fabry Disease in a Woman With Normal Enzyme Activity',
 'A woman with unexplained concentric LV hypertrophy has neuropathic pain and a suggestive maternal family history. Alpha-galactosidase A activity is within the laboratory reference range. Which next test most appropriately addresses suspected Fabry disease?',
 'GLA molecular genetic testing with appropriate variant interpretation',[('Repeat alpha-galactosidase A activity in leukocytes', 'Repeat testing can resolve a technical concern, but a normal value in a heterozygous woman does not reliably exclude Fabry. Molecular evaluation is needed when the clinical suspicion remains meaningful.'), ('Measure plasma lyso-Gb3 as the next confirmatory test', 'Lyso-Gb3 can support diagnosis and monitoring, particularly with a convincing pathogenic variant. Its sensitivity is limited in women and some later-onset phenotypes, so it is less definitive than GLA molecular evaluation in this woman.'), ('Obtain endomyocardial biopsy with electron microscopy', 'Tissue may clarify selected unresolved cases, but it is not the routine first confirmatory step for this clinically suggestive female presentation when GLA testing is available.')],
 [S('Recognize the Sex-Specific Diagnostic Limitation','**Fabry disease is an X-linked lysosomal disorder caused by deficient alpha-galactosidase A function.** Glycosphingolipid accumulation can affect myocardium, kidneys, peripheral nerves and other tissues.',
    'Heterozygous women can have important organ disease despite enzyme activity in the reference range. Molecular identification of a pathogenic or likely pathogenic GLA variant establishes the relevant genetic diagnosis; a VUS requires further interpretation.',
    'Review renal disease, neuropathic pain, sweating abnormalities, angiokeratomas, corneal findings and the pedigree, while recognizing that a cardiac-predominant phenotype may lack several classical features.'),
  S('Connect Diagnosis to Cardiac and Systemic Care','**CMR can show low native T1 from storage and inferolateral fibrosis, but neither finding alone establishes Fabry disease.** Fibrosis can alter the mapping appearance, and differential diagnoses remain important.',
    'Assess rhythm and conduction, ventricular function, renal status and cerebrovascular history. Evaluate relatives through genetic counseling after a convincing familial cause is identified.',
    'Disease-specific treatment can include enzyme replacement, such as agalsidase beta 1 mg/kg IV every two weeks with infusion-reaction monitoring. Migalastat 123 mg orally every other day is an option for appropriately selected adults with an amenable GLA variant; it is not interchangeable with enzyme replacement for every variant. Under the 2025 US label, migalastat is not recommended with eGFR below 30 mL/min/1.73 m². Take it without food or caffeine for two hours before and two hours after the dose; do not take it on consecutive days. Review variant amenability and pathogenicity separately before prescribing. Established scar may limit reversibility, so assessment should not wait for severe hypertrophy or advanced HF. Treat associated renal, rhythm and HF problems in parallel. The normal enzyme result in this woman changes the testing strategy; it does not close the diagnostic question.')],['FABRYGENE','CMP2023','AGALSIDASE','MIGALASTAT'])

R['TIF2025']=('Thalassaemia International Federation cardiovascular guidance, 2025','https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK614238/')
R['TIF2021']=('Thalassaemia International Federation transfusion-dependent thalassemia guideline summary, 2021','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9345633/')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Cardiac T2-Star and Myocardial Iron',
 'A transfusion-dependent patient has a validated 1.5-T myocardial T2* of 8 ms and LVEF 56%. Serum ferritin has fallen over recent months. Which interpretation should guide the next plan?',
 'Significant myocardial iron remains despite preserved EF; urgently review and intensify chelation through the specialist team',[
 ('Continue the existing chelation intensity and repeat cardiac CMR in two years','A two-year cardiac interval can be suitable for stable patients with T2* above twenty milliseconds. A value of eight milliseconds identifies a high-risk burden requiring earlier chelation review and closer reassessment even with preserved EF.'),
 ('Reduce chelation intensity in response to the improving ferritin trend','Dose reduction can prevent toxicity when the integrated iron assessment supports it. Ferritin is an imperfect systemic surrogate and cannot override severe myocardial loading measured directly by a validated cardiac acquisition.'),
 ('Adjust chelation according to liver iron before acting on the cardiac result','Liver iron informs systemic treatment and helps avoid excessive chelation. Cardiac and hepatic iron can clear at different rates; this directly abnormal myocardial result already justifies urgent cardiac-focused regimen assessment.')],
 [S('Interpret the Measurement Using the Validated Acquisition','**Iron accelerates magnetic signal decay, so lower myocardial T2* indicates greater iron loading.** The widely used clinical thresholds derive from validated 1.5-T methods and should not be transferred blindly to another field strength or sequence.',
    'T2* below twenty milliseconds supports myocardial iron; values below ten identify a particularly concerning burden with increased risk of ventricular deterioration and HF.',
    'Confirm image quality and the measurement method when a result conflicts with the clinical picture. Preserved EF does not negate tissue iron, and a normal ferritin trend is not a direct myocardial measurement.'),
  S('Act Before Overt Iron-Related Failure','**High cardiac iron requires prompt specialist review of chelation, adherence and transfusional iron input.** Tailor the regimen to cardiac burden, systemic iron, renal/hepatic status and tolerability.',
    'Intensive or combination chelation can be needed when burden is severe or cardiac dysfunction is present. Deferoxamine, deferiprone and deferasirox have distinct administration and toxicity profiles; they are not interchangeable milligram for milligram.',
    'Monitor blood counts for deferiprone-related neutropenia/agranulocytosis, renal and hepatic safety with deferasirox, and relevant infusion, ocular and auditory effects with deferoxamine. Reassess cardiac T2* and function at a severity-appropriate interval rather than using ferritin alone to decide when the heart is safe. Treat arrhythmia and HF concurrently if present. Phlebotomy can treat selected nonanemic iron-overload states but is not the routine iron-removal strategy for a transfusion-dependent anemic patient. The supplied value demands action even though systolic function has not yet fallen.')],['TIF2025','TIF2021','CMP2023'],
 table=(['Validated 1.5-T cardiac T2*','Usual interpretation'],[
 ['≥20 ms','No significant myocardial iron by this threshold'],
 ['10 to <20 ms','Myocardial iron present'],
 ['<10 ms','High-risk burden; urgent chelation review and close functional assessment']]))

R['EOS2026']=('ESC eosinophilic cardiac involvement clinical consensus, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1093/ejhf/xuag043')
add(SECTIONS[3][0],'Endomyocardial Fibrosis and Apical Obliteration',
 'A patient has restrictive physiology, marked atrial enlargement and apical cavity obliteration. CMR shows apical subendocardial enhancement with an overlying nonenhancing mural layer. Coronary anatomy does not explain the distribution. Which diagnosis best unifies the findings?',
 'Endomyocardial fibrosis with superimposed apical thrombus',[('Apical hypertrophic cardiomyopathy', 'Apical HCM can cause cavity narrowing and prominent T-wave abnormalities. The supplied endocardial fibrosis with an overlying nonenhancing layer favors a fibrotic-thrombotic process rather than treating the entire apparent apical mass as muscle.'), ('Chronic infarction with apical aneurysm and thrombus', 'Infarction can produce scar and thrombus, but a matching coronary distribution and aneurysmal remodeling would support that alternative. The supplied restrictive obliterative pattern and nonexplanatory coronaries favor endomyocardial disease.'), ('Cardiac amyloidosis with restrictive physiology', 'Amyloid can cause restrictive filling and wall thickening. Focal apical obliteration with an endocardial scar–thrombus configuration requires a different explanation and should not be assumed to be diffuse infiltration.')],
 [S('Distinguish Tissue Components Within the Apparent Mass','**Endomyocardial fibrosis can obliterate the ventricular apex and restrict filling.** Endocardial enhancement with overlying nonenhancing thrombus helps distinguish fibrosis and clot from hypertrophied myocardium.',
    'Atrial enlargement, atrioventricular valve involvement and pulmonary-pressure consequences can reflect the chronic filling abnormality. Disease may involve one or both ventricles.',
    'Use properly aligned echo and CMR views and relevant thrombus sequences. Apical foreshortening or treating every bright endocardial layer as myocardium can obscure the diagnosis.'),
  S('Look for Treatable Active Disease as Well as Chronic Fibrosis','**Eosinophilic cardiac injury can progress through overlapping inflammatory, thrombotic and fibrotic phases.** The 2026 ESC consensus emphasizes that these stages are not necessarily cleanly separated.',
    'Review eosinophil counts, medications, parasitic exposure, systemic inflammation and possible clonal hematologic disease. Absence of striking eosinophilia at one later visit does not prove there was no earlier eosinophilic injury.',
    'Treat documented thrombus and HF according to the clinical setting, and assess whether active eosinophilic inflammation requires urgent cause-directed treatment. Chronic tropical endomyocardial fibrosis should not automatically be equated with a currently active eosinophilic myocarditis. Persistent severe restrictive or valvular consequences can require surgical or advanced-HF assessment at an experienced center. Evaluate arrhythmia and embolic risk and reassess the response rather than assuming that anticoagulation will reverse established fibrosis. The diagnostic task is to identify the scar and thrombus components and then determine whether an ongoing inflammatory or systemic cause remains actionable.')],['CMP2023','EOS2026','MYO2025'])

R['MYOACC2024']=('ACC myocarditis diagnosis and management consensus pathway, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2024.10.080')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Suspected Myocarditis With an Acute Coronary Presentation',
 'A middle-aged patient has ongoing ischemic-type chest pain, regional ST elevation and a rising troponin. A febrile respiratory illness occurred ten days earlier. Which immediate diagnostic approach is most appropriate?',
 'Urgently assess and manage the possible acute coronary syndrome while evaluating myocarditis in parallel',[
 ('Obtain urgent CMR tissue characterization as the first definitive investigation','CMR is valuable once a time-sensitive coronary cause has been addressed. The recent infection does not exclude acute coronary occlusion; obtaining tissue characterization first can delay reperfusion in this ongoing regional ST-elevation presentation.'),
 ('Obtain endomyocardial biopsy as the first definitive investigation','Biopsy can identify a management-changing inflammatory subtype, particularly with shock, important ventricular arrhythmia or advanced block. It does not first exclude the urgent coronary explanation for this ischemic-type presentation.'),
 ('Obtain coronary CT angiography through the stable chest-pain pathway','CT coronary assessment is useful in selected stable patients with lower likelihood of obstructive coronary disease. Ongoing ischemic pain with regional ST elevation instead requires an urgent ACS pathway and invasive assessment when indicated.')],
 [S('Exclude a Time-Sensitive Competing Diagnosis','**Myocarditis can mimic acute MI, but a suggestive prodrome does not establish the diagnosis.** Ongoing ischemic symptoms, regional ST elevation and injury biomarkers require the appropriate acute-coronary assessment.',
    'Choose invasive or CT coronary assessment according to urgency, presentation and the applicable ACS pathway. Do not use a general rule for stable low-risk chest pain to delay treatment of a possible coronary occlusion.',
    'An unobstructed epicardial coronary study changes the differential but does not by itself prove myocarditis. Other ischemic mechanisms, Takotsubo and alternative myocardial injury require consideration.'),
  S('Build a Myocarditis Diagnosis From Clinical and Tissue Evidence','**ECG, troponin, echocardiography and CMR provide complementary information.** Normal initial testing does not reliably exclude disease when suspicion remains high, while an abnormal troponin is not etiologically specific.',
    'CMR can identify edema and nonischemic injury once the urgent competing problem has been addressed. Regional patterns, ventricular function and pericardial involvement help characterize the presentation.',
    'Assess hemodynamic and electrical risk alongside etiology. HF, sustained ventricular arrhythmia, high-grade AV block or worsening perfusion can require admission, advanced-center referral and selective biopsy rather than a routine outpatient scan. Review medication, immune therapy, systemic inflammatory and familial clues instead of assuming all presentations after a respiratory illness are viral. Document the working diagnosis and uncertainty, and update it as coronary and myocardial findings become available. The correct sequence protects against missing a treatable coronary emergency while preserving a full evaluation for inflammatory myocardial disease.')],['MYO2025','MYOACC2024'])

R['LLC2018']=('Updated CMR criteria for nonischemic myocardial inflammation, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jacc.2018.09.072')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Updated Lake Louise CMR Tissue Criteria',
 'In a clinically compatible suspected acute myocarditis presentation, which combination provides the strongest tissue-based support under the updated Lake Louise framework?',
 'At least one T2-based edema marker together with at least one T1-based myocardial-injury marker',[
 ('Two abnormal T1-based markers without any edema-sensitive finding','Multiple T1-based abnormalities may support myocardial injury, but they do not replace the complementary T2 component in the higher-specificity two-domain approach to acute inflammation.'),
 ('An isolated pericardial effusion together with a normal myocardial study','Pericardial involvement can support an inflammatory presentation, but an effusion is not a substitute for the required myocardial tissue domains. It can also arise from noninflammatory causes.'),
 ('Reduced global EF together with ventricular dilatation alone','Function and chamber size describe consequences and severity. They are not specific tissue markers and cannot independently distinguish myocarditis from other causes of cardiomyopathy.')],
 [S('Require Complementary Evidence of Edema and Injury','**The updated framework combines a T2-based domain with a T1-based domain.** The purpose is to increase specificity for acute myocardial inflammation in an appropriate clinical setting.',
    'Edema can be demonstrated by abnormal T2-weighted signal or elevated T2 mapping. Injury can be supported by abnormal native T1, increased extracellular volume or nonischemic LGE.',
    'Use sequence-, field-strength- and site-appropriate reference values. There is no universal millisecond cut-off that can be transferred across all scanners, and artefact must be excluded before calling a result positive.'),
  S('Interpret Timing and Diagnostic Limitations','**A positive CMR supports a clinical inflammatory diagnosis but does not identify the histological subtype or pathogen.** Giant-cell, eosinophilic, immune-mediated and inherited inflammatory presentations can need additional assessment.',
    'Diagnostic performance depends on timing and presentation. ESC 2025 notes higher accuracy early in the course, particularly within the first two weeks; arrhythmic or chronic presentations can be more challenging.',
    'One abnormal domain can still contribute supportive evidence, but the certainty is lower than with a concordant two-domain result. A negative study does not universally exclude patchy or early disease, and persistent LGE after edema resolves may represent residual injury rather than ongoing active inflammation. Pericardial abnormalities and ventricular dysfunction are supportive findings rather than replacements for myocardial criteria. Biopsy can be required when histology changes urgent management despite an informative scan. Correlate the result with symptoms, rhythm, biomarkers and the differential rather than equating any enhancement with active viral myocarditis.')],['LLC2018','MYO2025','MYOACC2024'],style='direct',
 table=(['CMR domain','Qualifying examples'],[
 ['T2-based edema','Elevated myocardial T2 or abnormal T2-weighted edema signal'],
 ['T1-based injury','Elevated native T1, increased ECV, or nonischemic LGE'],
 ['Supportive rather than tissue-domain replacement','Ventricular dysfunction or pericardial inflammation/effusion']]))

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Endomyocardial Biopsy in High-Risk Myocarditis',
 'An adult develops rapidly progressive new HF, sustained VT and high-grade AV block. Coronary obstruction has been excluded. The patient is being stabilized in an advanced cardiac center, and the team suspects a histological subtype requiring specific treatment. Which next etiological investigation is most appropriate?',
 'Urgent endomyocardial biopsy with expert histology, immunohistochemistry and appropriate molecular studies',[
 ('Contrast-enhanced CMR with mapping and late enhancement','CMR characterizes edema and myocardial injury and is often sufficient in a stable uncomplicated presentation. Here a dangerous electrical and HF phenotype raises an urgent histological question that imaging alone cannot settle.'),
 ('A serum cardiotropic-virus antibody panel','Serology can answer selected systemic exposure questions but generally does not establish myocardial infection. It cannot distinguish giant-cell, eosinophilic and other management-changing inflammatory histologies in this high-risk presentation.'),
 ('Prepared cardiac FDG-PET with a whole-body survey','PET can assess inflammatory activity and locate extracardiac biopsy targets, particularly in suspected sarcoidosis. It does not supply the myocardial histology needed to direct urgent treatment of the supplied high-risk syndrome.')],
 [S('Select Biopsy When Its Result Can Change Management','**Biopsy is particularly important when a high-risk inflammatory presentation may require etiology-directed treatment.** Rapid HF progression, sustained ventricular arrhythmia and high-grade conduction disease raise concern beyond an uncomplicated chest-pain syndrome.',
    'Giant-cell myocarditis, eosinophilic disease and other immune or inflammatory causes can require specific treatment. A tissue diagnosis can prevent both delayed therapy and indiscriminate immunosuppression for an unsupported cause.',
    'Arrange the investigation in an experienced center while maintaining perfusion, rhythm monitoring and necessary pacing or circulatory support. Stabilization should proceed in parallel rather than waiting for pathology.'),
  S('Obtain and Interpret Tissue Properly','**The biopsy plan includes sampling strategy and expert processing, not merely obtaining a fragment.** Histology, immunohistochemistry and appropriately handled molecular studies address different components.',
    'Patchy disease can produce false-negative samples. Imaging- or electroanatomically directed approaches and repeat sampling can be considered when clinical suspicion remains high and the result would change care.',
    'Interpret viral molecular findings with the full clinical and tissue context; systemic serology and myocardial genome detection are not interchangeable tests. CMR and PET can complement the investigation but do not identify every treatable histology from a pattern alone. Conversely, not every stable suspected myocarditis patient needs routine biopsy when a noninvasive diagnosis and conservative plan are appropriate. The indication here derives from the combination of severity and a management-changing etiological question. Communicate provisional results quickly to the treating team, and reassess the diagnosis if tissue and the clinical trajectory conflict.')],['MYO2025','MYOACC2024'])

R['MCS2023']=('ISHLT/HFSA acute mechanical circulatory support guideline, 2023','https://www.ishlt.org/docs/default-source/standards-guidelines/2023_guideline_jointstatement_ishlt_hfsa_mcsguideline.pdf')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Fulminant Myocarditis and Escalation of Circulatory Support',
 'A patient with suspected fulminant myocarditis has worsening lactate, oliguria and hypotension despite initial vasoactive support. Echo shows severe biventricular dysfunction. Which management sequence is most appropriate?',
 'Activate the shock team for temporary support and parallel etiological assessment',[
 ('Continue a pharmacological stabilization trial, reserving support referral for the next-day response','A monitored medication trial can be appropriate when perfusion responds and the patient remains stable. Rising lactate, oliguria and hypotension despite initial drugs indicate ongoing shock and justify immediate support-team escalation.'),
 ('Complete CMR and biopsy interpretation locally before arranging advanced-center transfer','Completing etiological assessment before transfer can be reasonable in a stable patient. Here progressive hypoperfusion makes diagnostic work parallel to rescue; imaging or pathology completion must not become a transfer prerequisite.'),
 ('Proceed directly to durable isolated LV support as the initial bridge strategy','Durable LV support is appropriate in selected advanced HF unlikely to recover with suitable RV function. Potentially reversible fulminant inflammation and severe biventricular failure favor initial temporary-support assessment with repeated recovery and RV evaluation.')],
 [S('Recognize a Clinical Severity Syndrome','**Fulminant myocarditis describes rapidly developing severe disease with hemodynamic compromise.** A high troponin or dramatic CMR alone does not define the need for circulatory rescue.',
    'Assess perfusion, lactate trend, renal and hepatic function, filling, oxygenation and arrhythmia. EF is only one component; deteriorating organ perfusion despite initial therapy is a critical escalation signal.',
    'Refer early to a center able to provide advanced HF, temporary support, biopsy and transplant assessment. Transfer logistics must preserve monitoring, airway and rescue capacity.'),
  S('Bridge to a Defined Next Decision','**Temporary MCS can bridge to myocardial recovery, a treatment response, durable support or transplantation.** Device choice depends on LV and RV failure, oxygenation, vascular access and the overall shock physiology.',
    'VA-ECMO can support severe circulatory collapse but can increase LV afterload; unloading and other support needs require active assessment. No one device is automatically the correct choice for every inflammatory shock presentation.',
    'Pursue timely biopsy when it can reveal a subtype needing specific treatment, while addressing arrhythmia and reversible contributors. Temporary improvement does not guarantee recovery, and a general assumption that fulminant disease has a favorable prognosis must not replace serial assessment. Reassess organ function, ventricular recovery and the definitive plan daily in the acute setting. Explain the purpose and risks of support to the family, including bleeding, thrombosis and vascular or infectious complications. The essential sequence is early recognition and coordinated rescue with diagnostic work proceeding alongside it, rather than using an arrest or irreversible multiorgan failure as the trigger to seek advanced care.')],['MYO2025','MYOACC2024','MCS2023'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Management of an Uncomplicated Myocarditis Presentation',
 'A patient has a clinically and CMR-supported acute myocarditis diagnosis after appropriate coronary assessment. EF is 60%, symptoms are improving, rhythm observation is reassuring and there is no HF, syncope or high-grade block. No specific immune, toxic or infectious treatment indication is identified. Which plan is most appropriate?',
 'Provide supportive care, activity restriction and scheduled rhythm/imaging reassessment',[('Begin prednisone plus azathioprine', 'This combination has randomized support in selected chronic biopsy-defined virus-negative inflammatory cardiomyopathy. The supplied improving acute presentation with preserved EF does not reproduce that population or establish a specific immune-treatment indication.'), ('Give intravenous immunoglobulin', 'IVIG has selected investigational or adjunctive roles, but evidence does not establish routine use in an uncomplicated adult myocarditis presentation. Supportive care and planned reassessment are appropriate without a specific indication.'), ('Obtain endomyocardial biopsy before choosing an observation plan', 'Biopsy is valuable when a high-risk presentation or a management-changing etiological question warrants tissue. This stable improving patient has no supplied HF or important electrical red flag requiring routine invasive investigation.')],
 [S('Match Treatment to the Actual Clinical Problem','**Uncomplicated myocarditis is managed according to risk, symptoms and any identified cause.** The absence of HF and important electrical instability distinguishes this presentation from a patient requiring shock support or urgent tissue-directed escalation.',
    'Treat congestion, ventricular dysfunction or arrhythmia if they develop, rather than prescribing an entire HF or antiarrhythmic regimen to every patient with a positive CMR. Review medication and exposure history and provide a clear plan for worsening symptoms.',
    'Anti-inflammatory analgesia can be considered for an appropriate chest-pain or associated pericardial phenotype. Do not generalize it to decompensated HF or assume that symptom relief establishes myocardial healing.'),
  S('Arrange Recovery Assessment Rather Than an Unsupervised Return','**Activity advice and follow-up should reflect the presentation and subsequent remission.** Symptoms, ventricular function, biomarkers, rhythm and tissue findings contribute different information.',
    'Avoid strenuous exercise during active disease and plan supervised reassessment before resuming intense activity. The precise timing should follow the applicable guideline and individual trajectory rather than an automatic return as soon as troponin falls.',
    'Explain urgent review for syncope, sustained palpitations, breathlessness at rest, edema or recurrent significant pain. A reassuring initial EF does not eliminate all later risk, although it contributes to the favorable assessment here. Immunosuppression, antivirals, biopsy and device treatment have selected indications and should not be triggered by the generic word myocarditis alone. Reassess the diagnosis if the course becomes recurrent or unexpectedly progressive, including possible inherited inflammatory cardiomyopathy or a systemic cause. Provide a defined follow-up plan so conservative treatment remains active clinical care rather than discharge without surveillance.')],['MYO2025','MYOACC2024'])

R['TIMIC']=('TIMIC randomized virus-negative inflammatory cardiomyopathy study, 2009','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehp249')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Selected Immunosuppression in Virus-Negative Inflammatory Cardiomyopathy',
 'A patient has persistent symptomatic LV dysfunction despite appropriate HF therapy for more than six months. Expert biopsy demonstrates lymphocytic inflammation and relevant myocardial viral testing is negative. Which added regimen was tested in TIMIC for this selected phenotype?',
 'Prednisone plus azathioprine',[('Prednisone plus cyclosporine', 'A calcineurin-inhibitor-based combination is used in giant-cell myocarditis and selected other immune conditions. TIMIC specifically tested prednisone plus azathioprine in chronic biopsy-proven virus-negative inflammatory cardiomyopathy; this alternative is not the randomized regimen asked about.'), ('Prednisone plus intravenous immunoglobulin', 'IVIG has been investigated in inflammatory myocardial disease, but this combination was not the TIMIC regimen. Its potential use in another clinical context should not be mistaken for direct randomized support in the supplied selected chronic phenotype.'), ('Mycophenolate without prednisone', 'Mycophenolate has selected steroid-sparing or additional-treatment roles in immune-mediated disease. TIMIC tested combined prednisone and azathioprine rather than mycophenolate monotherapy; the named trial cannot establish equivalence of these regimens.')],
 [S('Apply the Evidence to the Studied Population','**TIMIC randomized 85 patients with biopsy-proven virus-negative inflammatory cardiomyopathy and chronic HF unresponsive to conventional treatment.** Its findings support selected treatment rather than routine immunosuppression for an undifferentiated clinical label.',
    'The study population had LV dysfunction and a prolonged symptomatic course. This differs from uncomplicated acute myocarditis with preserved function or an untyped fulminant presentation.',
    'A tissue-based etiological assessment matters because lymphocytic inflammation, giant-cell disease, eosinophilic injury and an active infectious process can require different strategies. Do not assume that one serum test defines all of them.'),
  S('Use an Explicit Regimen and Safety Plan','**The trial used prednisone 1 mg/kg/day for four weeks, then 0.33 mg/kg/day for five months, together with azathioprine 2 mg/kg/day for six months.** This is a reference study regimen requiring individual clinical review.',
    'Assess infection risk, blood counts, liver function, renal status and azathioprine-metabolism susceptibility according to local testing practice. Monitor marrow and hepatic toxicity and review major interactions, including xanthine-oxidase inhibitors.',
    'Monitor steroid-associated infection, glucose, pressure, fluid retention and bone risk; prophylaxis and tapering depend on the overall immunosuppressive exposure and patient factors. Continue HF treatment and reassess symptoms, function and rhythm rather than using reduced inflammatory markers as the sole endpoint. Treatment should be coordinated by a team able to interpret biopsy and molecular findings and respond to adverse effects. The point is not that negative viral testing automatically mandates steroids, but that a carefully defined persistent inflammatory phenotype has evidence for an additional therapeutic strategy.')],['TIMIC','MYO2025','MYOACC2024'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Giant-Cell Myocarditis: Combined Immunosuppression',
 'A patient with rapidly progressive HF, VT and AV block has biopsy-confirmed giant-cell myocarditis. Perfusion and rhythm support are being provided. Which disease-directed strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Corticosteroids plus a calcineurin inhibitor, with advanced-HF assessment',[('Corticosteroids as the initial disease-directed monotherapy', 'Steroids are a component of treatment, but alone have inferior supporting outcomes compared with combined immunosuppression in giant-cell disease. Early symptomatic improvement is not evidence that an additional agent is unnecessary.'), ('HF and rhythm therapy with reassessment before adding immune treatment', 'HF treatment is essential but does not address the rapidly destructive immune process. A confirmed giant-cell subtype creates a reason for prompt disease-directed therapy rather than a prolonged conventional-treatment trial.'), ('Corticosteroids combined with intravenous immunoglobulin', 'IVIG has been studied as an immunomodulatory strategy in selected inflammatory myocardial disorders. It does not reproduce the evidence-supported calcineurin-inhibitor-based combined approach for biopsy-confirmed giant-cell myocarditis.')],
 [S('Recognize a High-Risk Histological Diagnosis','**Giant-cell myocarditis can cause rapidly progressive ventricular failure, malignant ventricular arrhythmia and advanced conduction disease.** The biopsy result changes management beyond a generic clinical myocarditis diagnosis.',
    'Distinguish it from sarcoid and other inflammatory histologies through expert pathology. Patchy disease can make a first sample nondiagnostic, so persistent strong suspicion may justify repeat or directed sampling.',
    'Treat perfusion and electrical instability concurrently with immunosuppression. A need for temporary support or transplant evaluation is not a reason to postpone the disease-directed plan.'),
  S('Combine Treatment and Monitor Its Consequences','**Regimens commonly combine corticosteroids with cyclosporine or another specialist-selected agent, often with azathioprine or mycophenolate.** Evidence and guidance favor a combined approach over steroids alone.',
    'ESC 2025 lists severe-disease methylprednisolone 7–14 mg/kg/day IV for three days, followed by oral prednisone approximately 1 mg/kg/day with subsequent specialist taper and additional immunosuppression. Select and monitor the accompanying regimen individually.',
    'Calcineurin inhibitors require trough-level, renal, pressure and interaction surveillance. Antimetabolites require blood-count, hepatic and infection monitoring, and steroid toxicity prevention must reflect the total exposure. Do not abruptly withdraw treatment solely because EF improves; relapse can occur and long-term management is specialized. Reassess ventricular and arrhythmic status and the need for durable support or transplantation. Transplantation can be lifesaving, but surveillance remains necessary because recurrence in the graft is possible. An ICD or pacemaker treats specific rhythm consequences rather than the underlying inflammatory destruction. The appropriate response is a coordinated immune, HF and arrhythmia strategy, not a single drug or device presented as a cure.')],['MYO2025','MYOACC2024'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Hypersensitivity Eosinophilic Myocarditis',
 'Three weeks after a new antibiotic, a patient develops rash, eosinophilia and acute LV dysfunction. Biopsy shows eosinophilic myocarditis. The team considers drug hypersensitivity the likely cause after directed assessment. Which initial disease-directed strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Withdraw the drug and initiate systemic glucocorticoids',[('Withdraw the drug and reassess LV function before starting immune treatment', 'Withdrawal is necessary and can be sufficient for selected mild drug reactions. Established myocardial injury with important dysfunction creates a different risk setting and warrants prompt specialist treatment rather than observation alone.'), ('Withdraw the drug and initiate azathioprine', 'Azathioprine has selected steroid-sparing uses, but it is not the usual rapidly acting initial treatment for acute hypersensitivity myocardial inflammation. Drug withdrawal and corticosteroid-based induction address the immediate process.'), ('Withdraw the drug and initiate an anti-IL-5 biological agent', 'Anti-IL-5 therapy has selected uses in EGPA, hypereosinophilic syndromes or steroid-sparing treatment. It is not the usual initial induction strategy for clinically important acute drug-hypersensitivity myocarditis; withdraw the culprit and promptly control inflammation with glucocorticoids.')],
 [S('Identify and Remove the Cause','**Eosinophilic myocarditis can result from drug hypersensitivity, EGPA, hypereosinophilic syndromes, infection or hematologic disease.** A rash and recent exposure are clues, not substitutes for evaluating alternatives.',
    'Withdraw the likely culprit and document the reaction. Review other organ involvement and obtain directed testing for parasitic, vasculitic and clonal causes when appropriate.',
    'Peripheral eosinophilia can be absent or variable, while tissue can show clinically important disease. Conversely, eosinophilia alone does not prove that cardiac symptoms are due to myocardial eosinophilic infiltration.'),
  S('Treat Inflammation, Thrombosis and Hemodynamics Separately','**Systemic corticosteroids are commonly used for clinically consequential hypersensitivity myocarditis alongside culprit withdrawal.** Severity determines the route and intensity, with monitored expert reassessment.',
    'For fulminant eosinophilic myocarditis, the ESC 2026 consensus advises methylprednisolone 7–14 mg/kg/day IV, maximum 1000 mg/day, for three days, followed by approximately 1 mg/kg/day and a clinical-response-guided taper.',
    'Assess Strongyloides and other parasite risk before or alongside urgent immunosuppression; when Strongyloides cannot be excluded, specialist-directed antiparasitic treatment may be needed to prevent hyperinfection. Treat documented intracardiac thrombus and consider the wider thromboembolic context, but do not treat anticoagulation as an anti-inflammatory substitute. Provide HF and rhythm care and escalate circulatory support when perfusion fails. Steroid-refractory disease or a defined EGPA or clonal cause can require additional targeted therapy. Long-term anti-IL-5 treatment is not automatically the induction regimen for every life-threatening cardiac presentation. Follow organ recovery and eosinophilic activity rather than judging success only from rash resolution.')],['EOS2026','MYO2025','MYOACC2024'])

R['ONCOESC2022']=('ESC cardio-oncology guideline, 2022','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehac244')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Immune-Checkpoint-Inhibitor Myocarditis: Immediate Treatment',
 'Soon after combination checkpoint-inhibitor therapy, a patient develops troponin elevation, new complete AV block and ventricular ectopy. Initial EF is 58%. The clinical team considers ICI myocarditis likely while investigating alternative causes. Under the ESC cardio-oncology approach, which immediate strategy is appropriate?',
 'Interrupt ICI, admit with rhythm support and initiate pulse IV methylprednisolone',[('Interrupt ICI and provide pacing, introducing steroids if LV dysfunction develops', 'Dangerous ICI myocarditis can occur with preserved EF. New block and myocardial injury require urgent action; EF decline is not a prerequisite for interrupting treatment and beginning the indicated evaluation and therapy.'), ('Interrupt ICI and provide monitored support while awaiting biopsy before steroids', 'Tissue can be important when uncertainty remains, but ESC recommends prompt treatment once the diagnosis is considered likely. Delay in a high-risk electrical presentation can be dangerous.'), ('Interrupt ICI, admit with rhythm monitoring and initiate oral prednisone 1 mg/kg/day', 'Oral prednisone around this dose is used in some other immune-toxicity pathways and after initial pulse treatment when response permits. ESC 2022 recommends prompt high-dose IV methylprednisolone for likely ICI myocarditis; complete AV block reinforces the need for intensive treatment and monitoring.')],
 [S('Recognize Electrical Toxicity Despite a Preserved EF','**ICI myocarditis may cause severe conduction disease and ventricular arrhythmia without marked global systolic impairment.** Evaluate coronary, infectious and other competing causes while addressing the dangerous presentation.',
    'Review associated myositis and myasthenia-like features, including bulbar or respiratory weakness. Dyspnea can reflect neuromuscular toxicity as well as HF, and overlap can change immediate monitoring needs.',
    'Interrupt the checkpoint inhibitor and coordinate oncology, cardiology and critical care. Continuous ECG monitoring and rapid access to pacing and defibrillation are essential for this supplied phenotype.'),
  S('Start Prompt Immunosuppression and Reassess Early','**ESC cardio-oncology 2022 recommends methylprednisolone 500–1000 mg IV daily for the first three to five days once ICI myocarditis is considered likely.** This named regimen should not be confused with a low-dose outpatient approach.',
    'If clinical and biomarker response is adequate, transition to an individualized oral steroid taper with surveillance. Persistent troponin elevation, block, arrhythmia or dysfunction requires early assessment for steroid resistance and additional immunomodulation.',
    'Selected refractory or severe cases may receive agents such as mycophenolate or abatacept through expert care; the evidence and sequencing differ between guidance and evolving practice. Do not represent every adjunct as equally established or extrapolate a fixed regimen to all patients. Review infection and steroid complications and provide hemodynamic support when needed. Rechallenge with cancer immunotherapy requires multidisciplinary reassessment of recovery, recurrence risk and cancer alternatives; it is not automatic after troponin normalizes. A technically successful pacemaker treats one consequence, while the myocardial immune process still needs management.')],['ONCOESC2022','MYO2025','MYOACC2024'])

R['SARCOIDESC2024']=('ESC multidisciplinary cardiac sarcoidosis clinical consensus, 2024','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehae356')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Complementary Imaging and Tissue Assessment in Cardiac Sarcoidosis',
 'A patient with biopsy-confirmed extracardiac sarcoidosis develops ventricular arrhythmia. CMR shows patchy nonischemic LGE, while properly prepared cardiac FDG-PET demonstrates focal activity. An unguided myocardial biopsy is negative. Which interpretation is most appropriate?',
 'Integrate the extracardiac histology, clinical phenotype and complementary imaging; a negative unguided biopsy does not exclude cardiac involvement',[('Regard myocardial involvement as unconfirmed until a repeat cardiac biopsy is positive', 'Patchy involvement limits the sensitivity of unguided endomyocardial biopsy. A negative sample does not overrule a compatible systemic, electrical and imaging assessment.'), ('Use persistent LGE burden as the principal measure of current inflammatory activity', 'LGE can reflect persistent scar and does not independently establish activity. PET and the clinical trajectory provide complementary information rather than being interchangeable with enhancement.'), ('Assign a definite myocardial histological subtype from the focal PET distribution', 'Focal PET activity can support an inflammatory diagnosis and identify biopsy targets. It is not histologically specific, so it cannot independently establish the tissue subtype; systemic histology, phenotype and complementary imaging must be integrated.')],
 [S('Ask Different Tests Different Questions','**CMR defines myocardial structure, function and tissue injury; FDG-PET can assess inflammatory metabolic activity.** Their findings can be concordant or reflect different phases of disease.',
    'PET requires preparation to suppress physiological myocardial uptake. Diffuse uptake after inadequate preparation can create an equivocal or misleading result rather than proving widespread inflammation.',
    'No single LGE distribution uniquely establishes sarcoidosis. Ischemia, other inflammatory disorders and inherited cardiomyopathies can overlap and should be considered when findings do not fit.'),
  S('Apply a Named Diagnostic Framework','**Clinical diagnostic pathways can support cardiac involvement without positive myocardial histology when qualifying extracardiac tissue and cardiac findings are present.** Different society criteria have distinct requirements; avoid inventing a universal one-test rule.',
    'An accessible abnormal extracardiac site can provide useful tissue. If myocardial sampling is necessary, imaging or electroanatomic guidance can improve yield, although a negative result still needs contextual interpretation.',
    'Separate the certainty of diagnosis from the urgency of treating VT, block or HF. Inflammation-directed treatment, antiarrhythmics and device protection address different problems. Reassess imaging and clinical status when response is unclear, and reconsider giant-cell or genetic disease when the phenotype is discordant. A reassuring EF does not eliminate arrhythmic risk, while positive imaging alone does not make every asymptomatic person an automatic immunosuppression candidate. The supplied tissue-proven systemic disease and convergent cardiac findings justify integrated assessment despite one negative unguided myocardial sample.')],['SARCOID2024','SARCOIDESC2024','MYO2025'])

add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Cardiac Sarcoidosis: Inflammation and Device Protection',
 'A patient with established active cardiac sarcoidosis has persistent complete AV block requiring permanent pacing, LVEF 52% and myocardial scar. Meaningful survival beyond one year is expected. Which plan best integrates disease treatment and rhythm protection?',
 'Treat inflammation and consider an ICD with pacing capability',[('Treat inflammation and implant a conventional dual-chamber pacemaker', 'A conventional pacemaker treats persistent AV block and may be appropriate when defibrillation is not indicated or chosen. Sarcoidosis with a pacing indication and scar warrants assessment of an ICD-capable system despite EF above thirty-five percent.'), ('Treat inflammation with temporary pacing, deferring a permanent-system decision for several weeks', 'Temporary pacing is useful during initial stabilization or a short diagnostic interval. Recovery of AV conduction with immune therapy is uncertain, and persistent complete block should not be left on a prolonged temporary strategy without a dependable permanent protection plan.'), ('Treat inflammation and implant a CRT pacemaker as the default permanent system', 'CRT can benefit selected patients with ventricular dysfunction and an appropriate electrical or anticipated pacing context. EF fifty-two percent and a pacing indication do not automatically establish a CRT indication, and CRT-P alone does not provide defibrillation protection.')],
 [S('Do Not Use LV EF as the Only Device Criterion','**Cardiac sarcoidosis can confer ventricular-arrhythmia risk in a patient who presents primarily with AV block.** A permanent pacing requirement is relevant to ICD assessment even when EF exceeds thirty-five percent.',
    'The AHA 2024 statement describes the US class IIa pathway for EF above thirty-five with an indication for permanent pacing, relevant syncope or scar. Confirm the applicable framework rather than describing every such patient as an unconditional class I case.',
    'Select the system after evaluating anticipated pacing burden, conduction pattern, ventricular function, ATP needs and patient preferences. An ICD indication does not automatically establish a CRT indication.'),
  S('Treat Active Inflammation and Monitor Both Outcomes','**Corticosteroids are commonly the initial immune treatment for clinically active cardiac sarcoidosis.** The AHA statement uses prednisone-equivalent initiation around 30–40 mg/day with individualized taper and escalation.',
    'Steroid-sparing options include methotrexate, mycophenolate and azathioprine. ESC 2024 lists methotrexate 15–25 mg once weekly with folic acid; review blood counts, hepatic/renal function, infection risk and reproductive contraindications.',
    'Life-threatening inflammatory presentations can need pulse treatment and more intensive care. Follow clinical status, rhythm, ventricular function and appropriately selected repeat inflammatory imaging; no single biomarker settles every treatment decision. Scar-related risk can persist when PET activity resolves, and immunosuppression cannot be assumed to eliminate an independent device indication. Conversely, a well-functioning device is not evidence that inflammation is controlled. Explain the separate aims of preventing bradycardia, terminating dangerous ventricular rhythms and treating active myocardial disease.')],['SARCOID2024','SARCOIDESC2024','MYO2025'])

R['MYORHYTHM2026']=('International myocarditis and inflammatory-cardiomyopathy rhythm consensus, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1093/europace/euag153')
add(SECTIONS[4][0],'Myocarditis Recovery and Return to Intense Exercise',
 'Three months after acute myocarditis, a patient is pain-free with normal troponin and LVEF 59%. CMR still shows nonischemic scar, and supervised rhythm assessment documents exercise-associated NSVT. The patient wants clearance for endurance competition. Which plan is most appropriate?',
 'Defer intense exercise clearance and investigate the residual arrhythmic risk through individualized specialist reassessment',[('Permit graded return to competition after the present normal-EF assessment', 'Mechanical and biomarker recovery are reassuring but do not neutralize documented exercise-associated ventricular arrhythmia. Recovery assessment must include the electrical phenotype.'), ('Extend exercise restriction to six months and then clear competition on elapsed time', 'Calendar time is not a substitute for clinical, structural and rhythm assessment. The supplied ongoing arrhythmia requires attention regardless of reaching a commonly quoted interval.'), ('Suppress NSVT with a beta blocker and permit competition once symptoms remain absent', 'Beta blockade can suppress selected ventricular arrhythmias and symptoms. Suppression or absence of symptoms does not by itself resolve the risk associated with exercise-triggered NSVT and residual scar; specialist reassessment must precede competitive clearance.')],
 [S('Assess Remission Across Multiple Domains','**Return to intense activity after myocarditis requires more than symptom and troponin improvement.** Evaluate ventricular function, rhythm, clinical course and residual tissue abnormalities.',
    'ESC 2025 recommends restricting activity in the acute phase and individualizing return according to remission. Exercise testing and ambulatory monitoring can reveal clinically important residual arrhythmia that a resting ECG misses.',
    'Persistent LGE can represent healed scar rather than continuing active inflammation. It does not automatically mean permanent exercise prohibition, but its distribution and associated symptoms or arrhythmia influence the assessment.'),
  S('Act on the Residual Electrical Phenotype','**Exercise-associated NSVT in this recovery setting deserves a defined risk evaluation.** Review its morphology, burden, symptoms and relationship to scar, and reassess whether active inflammation or an inherited substrate remains relevant.',
    'The 2026 rhythm consensus emphasizes disease phase, substrate and tailored assessment rather than relying on global EF alone. Repeat CMR around six months can contribute to the overall follow-up plan when appropriate.',
    'Choose additional monitoring or specialist electrophysiological assessment for a specific clinical question; neither every brief run nor every scar automatically mandates an ICD. Avoid resuming high-intensity endurance training while a concerning documented finding remains unresolved. Provide an individualized lower-intensity plan and clear instructions for syncope, sustained palpitations or recurrent symptoms. Distinguish the return-to-activity decision from a device prescription and from the question of whether immunosuppression is still needed. The supplied normal EF and biomarker result are part of the evidence, but the observed ventricular arrhythmia prevents treating them as complete exercise clearance.')],['MYO2025','MYOACC2024','MYORHYTHM2026'])

R['PREG2025']=('ESC cardiovascular disease and pregnancy guideline, 2025','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehaf193')
R['PPCM2026']=('ESC HFA peripartum cardiomyopathy access and care consensus, 2026','https://doi.org/10.1093/ejhf/xuag024')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Diagnosis of Peripartum Cardiomyopathy',
 'Two weeks after delivery, a previously well woman develops orthopnea and pulmonary congestion. Echo shows global LV dysfunction with EF 32%, without predominant RV strain or a regional ballooning pattern. BP and directed evaluation reveal no adequate alternative cause. Which diagnosis is best supported?',
 'Peripartum cardiomyopathy',[('Acute pulmonary embolism', 'PE is an important postpartum differential and requires investigation when clinically suspected. Predominant new global LV systolic dysfunction without the supplied RV-strain pattern better supports the cardiomyopathic presentation here.'), ('Peripartum Takotsubo syndrome', 'Physical stress can trigger Takotsubo, but a trigger alone does not establish it. The supplied global dysfunction lacks the characteristic regional pattern needed to support that alternative.'), ('Acute hypertensive heart disease', 'Hypertensive pregnancy disorders can cause pulmonary edema and cardiac dysfunction. The supplied blood pressure and directed work-up do not support that mechanism; unexplained peripartum systolic dysfunction instead supports PPCM.')],
 [S('Use Timing, Function and Exclusion of Other Causes','**PPCM is HF with LVEF below 45% occurring mainly around the peripartum period or in the months following pregnancy, without another adequate cause.** ESC 2025 includes presentations after delivery, termination or miscarriage.',
    'It is not restricted to patients whose EF is below thirty-five percent. LV dilatation can occur but is not required for the diagnosis.',
    'A normal pregnancy history does not make postpartum dyspnea physiological. Orthopnea, pulmonary edema, persistent tachycardia or signs of poor perfusion warrant prompt evaluation.'),
  S('Investigate Competing and Contributing Conditions','**Assess PE, hypertensive disease, ischemia including pregnancy-associated coronary pathology, valve disease and other cardiomyopathy causes.** Echo, ECG, biomarkers and targeted further imaging answer complementary questions.',
    'Inherited susceptibility can overlap with PPCM, including pathogenic variants encountered in DCM. Genetic counseling and testing can be appropriate rather than assuming the peripartum label proves an exclusively acquired disorder.',
    'Assess ventricular thrombus, rhythm and severity while initiating appropriate HF care through a pregnancy-heart or cardio-obstetric team. Some patients recover substantially, but recovery cannot be guaranteed from age or timing. Severe dysfunction can require temporary support and a staged arrhythmic-protection decision while function is reassessed. Document the baseline EF and follow-up plan and explain future pregnancy and contraception implications. The diagnosis unifies the temporal presentation and unexplained systolic dysfunction; it is not a reason to skip urgent investigation when an alternative postpartum emergency is clinically plausible.')],['PREG2025','PPCM2026','CMP2023'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'HFrEF Medication During an Ongoing Pregnancy',
 'At 30 weeks of pregnancy, a patient with new systolic HF has been stabilized. She remains mildly congested, with adequate BP and no shock or contraindication to beta blockade. Which continuing drug strategy is compatible with pregnancy?',
 'A suitable beta-1-selective blocker, cautious loop diuresis and hydralazine/nitrate afterload treatment when needed',[
 ('A beta blocker, loop diuretic and enalapril as the initial antenatal regimen','Enalapril can be useful after delivery, including selected breastfeeding settings. ACE inhibition is contraindicated during pregnancy and should not be transferred unchanged from the postpartum regimen.'),
 ('A beta blocker, loop diuretic and sacubitril/valsartan as the initial antenatal regimen','ARNI treatment is an established HFrEF option outside pregnancy. The renin–angiotensin-system effects and fetal safety concerns preclude using this as the supplied pregnancy-compatible plan.'),
 ('A beta blocker, loop diuretic and an SGLT2 inhibitor as the initial antenatal regimen','SGLT2 inhibitors have major HFrEF benefits in eligible nonpregnant adults. That evidence does not establish fetal safety or a routine pregnancy indication, and this class is avoided during pregnancy.')],
 [S('Adapt HF Treatment to Pregnancy Physiology and Safety','**Pregnancy-compatible therapy can include a suitable beta blocker, diuresis for congestion and hydralazine with nitrates for needed afterload reduction.** Titrate according to maternal perfusion and fetal considerations.',
    'Introduce or continue beta blockade after stabilization rather than loading it during uncontrolled shock. Monitor maternal rate and pressure and assess fetal growth; agents are not interchangeable, and atenolol is generally avoided.',
    'Use diuretics to treat genuine congestion without causing excessive volume depletion or compromising placental perfusion. Persistent pulmonary edema or poor output requires escalation rather than repeated outpatient dose changes.'),
  S('Revisit the Regimen After Delivery','**ACE inhibitors, ARBs, ARNIs, MRAs and SGLT2 inhibitors are not the routine antenatal HFrEF regimen.** Their use after delivery depends on maternal indication and the specific breastfeeding safety evidence.',
    'Selected postpartum options, including enalapril and spironolactone, can be compatible with breastfeeding in an appropriate patient. Do not assume that all members of a class share identical lactation evidence or that every pre-pregnancy medicine can be restarted without review.',
    'A multidisciplinary team should address delivery planning, anticoagulation indications, rhythm, renal function and recovery surveillance. The requirement to avoid particular drugs during pregnancy is not a reason to leave HF untreated. Likewise, symptom improvement does not justify stopping follow-up while ventricular dysfunction persists. Explain which drugs are being deferred and when the plan will be reconsidered, including the interaction between lactation decisions and disease-specific treatments. New hypotension, hypoxemia or organ hypoperfusion changes the care setting and may require urgent advanced support.')],['PREG2025','PPCM2026'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Bromocriptine in Peripartum Cardiomyopathy',
 'A postpartum patient with PPCM is receiving appropriate HF therapy. The team discusses adding bromocriptine. Which statement best reflects ESC 2025 guidance?',
 'Consider adjunctive treatment with lactation counseling and at least prophylactic LMWH',[('Add it to HF therapy and use therapeutic anticoagulation throughout its course', 'Therapeutic-dose anticoagulation is appropriate with ventricular thrombus, an embolic event or another established indication. Bromocriptine guidance calls for considering at least prophylactic LMWH, rather than prescribing therapeutic intensity to every recipient.'), ('Add it to HF therapy and use thromboprophylaxis only if EF remains below thirty-five percent', 'Severe LV dysfunction adds thromboembolic concern, but the bromocriptine-associated thromboprophylaxis discussion is not confined to EF below thirty-five percent. Consider bleeding risk, the adjunct and independent anticoagulation indications together.'), ('Defer it because its potential role is limited to patients with persistent hyperprolactinemia', 'PPCM use targets a proposed prolactin-related injury pathway and is not restricted to proven persistent hyperprolactinemia. It may be considered as an adjunct after a benefit, uncertainty, lactation and safety discussion.')],
 [S('Understand the Proposed Mechanism and Evidence','**Bromocriptine is a dopamine agonist that suppresses prolactin and stops lactation.** Its PPCM rationale involves potentially harmful prolactin-related pathways, but not every case has an identical mechanism.',
    'ESC 2025 gives an adjunctive may-be-considered recommendation for improving LV recovery. Discuss uncertainty and local practice instead of describing the drug as universally required.',
    'A trial comparing a one-week course with a longer eight-week course did not show a significant difference between those durations. A comparison of two active schedules does not by itself establish placebo-controlled mortality benefit.'),
  S('Use a Complete Specialist Treatment Plan','**The 2025 ESC guidance advises considering at least prophylactic LMWH when bromocriptine is used.** Stronger anticoagulation can be needed for a separate indication such as documented ventricular thrombus.',
    'Discuss the effect on breastfeeding, the feeding plan and maternal preferences. Monitor pressure and vascular or thrombotic concerns and review contraindications; dose and duration should be selected for the particular clinical setting.',
    'Published PPCM schedules include 2.5 mg daily for one week or 5 mg daily for two weeks followed by 2.5 mg daily for six weeks. The acute severe-HF algorithm lists a starting 2.5 mg twice daily approach with titration; these are distinct contexts, not one interchangeable universal prescription. Continue HF care and recovery assessment, and do not use prolactin suppression as proof that ventricular or arrhythmic risk has resolved. Explain the purpose of the adjunct, its uncertainties and the accompanying safety plan so consent reflects more than an expectation of a guaranteed EF recovery.')],['PREG2025','PPCM2026'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Subsequent Pregnancy After Recovery From PPCM',
 'A woman with previous PPCM now has no HF symptoms and LVEF 56% on repeated studies. She requests counseling before another pregnancy. Which approach is most appropriate?',
 'Arrange preconception team assessment and counsel about residual recurrence risk',[('Offer pregnancy clearance after documenting stable EF above fifty percent', 'EF recovery is reassuring but does not describe all residual risk. Review the previous severity, current function, treatment, rhythm and clinical trajectory before making a pregnancy plan.'), ('Defer counseling until a cardiomyopathy panel establishes an inherited cause', 'A genetic result can refine diagnosis and family counseling, but a negative or pending panel does not remove recurrence risk. Clinical preconception assessment and reproductive counseling should proceed without waiting for a molecular cause.'), ('Withdraw HF therapy before referral to establish whether recovery is durable', 'Medication changes require a monitored plan and consideration of both relapse and fetal drug safety. Abrupt indiscriminate withdrawal is not an appropriate substitute for preconception assessment.')],
 [S('Counsel Even After Apparent Recovery','**ESC 2025 recommends recurrence-risk and contraception counseling after PPCM even when EF has recovered above 50%.** Recovery improves the risk profile but is not equivalent to absence of future risk.',
    'Persistent dysfunction creates greater concern and can make pregnancy strongly inadvisable under the applicable maternal-risk framework. A recovered patient still needs an individual discussion rather than automatic reassurance.',
    'Review ventricular size and function, exercise tolerance, symptoms, arrhythmia, previous shock or thromboembolism and the need for ongoing therapy. Consider genetic counseling where appropriate.'),
  S('Plan Medication, Surveillance and Reproductive Care','**A preconception pregnancy-heart team can coordinate safe medication changes and monitoring.** Drugs with fetal risk must be addressed before conception without assuming that every beneficial therapy should be stopped at once.',
    'When a reversible course is assumed, ESC 2025 supports considering HF treatment for at least twelve months after complete LV recovery before subsequent management is individualized. Recovery includes ventricular volumes and EF, not a single improved number.',
    'Discuss effective contraception until the plan is settled, the expected pregnancy and postpartum follow-up, delivery considerations and symptoms requiring urgent review. If a subsequent pregnancy proceeds, surveillance should continue through the postpartum period, when recurrence may present. Explain the limitations of predicting an individual outcome from registry averages or one normal stress test. Shared decision-making should preserve the patient’s reproductive goals while conveying the possibility of recurrent HF despite a reassuring current EF. A molecular diagnosis or negative genetic result can refine counseling but does not replace the clinical assessment.')],['PREG2025','PPCM2026','CMP2023'])

R['TAK1']=('International Takotsubo consensus: diagnostic criteria, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehy076')
R['TAK2']=('International Takotsubo consensus: work-up and management, 2018','https://doi.org/10.1093/eurheartj/ehy077')
R['TAK2024']=('International Takotsubo practical-challenges consensus, 2024','https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11589216/')
add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Takotsubo Diagnostic Criteria and Coronary Disease',
 'Which statement is consistent with the InterTAK diagnostic criteria?',
 'Significant coronary disease can coexist with Takotsubo and does not automatically exclude it',[('An emotional or physical trigger is a diagnostic prerequisite', 'Triggers are common but not required under InterTAK. A convincing transient ventricular pattern and appropriate exclusion of alternatives can support the diagnosis without a recalled precipitating stressor.'), ('Pheochromocytoma is an exclusion criterion', 'Some older criteria treated pheochromocytoma as an exclusion. InterTAK recognizes it as a potential catecholamine-related trigger, so the answer depends on the named diagnostic framework.'), ('A wall-motion pattern confined to one coronary territory excludes the diagnosis', 'Takotsubo dysfunction usually extends beyond one epicardial vascular territory. InterTAK permits focal exceptions, which require careful differentiation from infarction and myocarditis using coronary assessment, CMR and the clinical course.')],
 [S('Use the Updated Framework Rather Than an Older Exclusion List','**InterTAK allows coexisting coronary disease when the overall evidence supports Takotsubo.** Coronary anatomy must still be examined carefully so a treatable culprit lesion is not dismissed.',
    'Typical patterns include transient apical, midventricular, basal or focal dysfunction, sometimes involving the RV. The wall-motion distribution usually extends beyond one epicardial territory, although focal exceptions occur.',
    'A neurological event or pheochromocytoma can be a trigger, and an emotional or physical trigger is not mandatory. Postmenopausal women predominate, but sex and age are not absolute diagnostic requirements.'),
  S('Exclude Important Mimics and Document the Course','**An acute coronary-like presentation must initially be assessed through the appropriate urgent pathway.** A ballooning pattern is not permission to withhold indicated coronary evaluation.',
    'CMR helps distinguish nonischemic inflammation and infarction and can support the diagnosis when the presentation is uncertain. Follow-up demonstration of recovery is important, while an acute severe complication may occur before recovery can be documented.',
    'ECG abnormalities and a biomarker rise are common but neither is unique. Assess QT, arrhythmia, LVOTO, mitral regurgitation and thrombus rather than treating a potentially reversible syndrome as harmless. A typical stress story is supportive context, not proof, and an incidental coronary lesion is not necessarily the entire explanation. Review the pattern’s relationship to the artery, tissue findings and clinical evolution. Distinguish diagnostic criteria from a prediction that a patient will recover without complications or that a single drug prevents recurrence.')],['TAK1','TAK2','TAK2024'],style='direct')

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Takotsubo Shock With Dynamic Outflow Obstruction',
 'A patient with Takotsubo develops hypotension. Echo shows apical dysfunction, vigorous basal contraction, systolic anterior mitral motion and a severe dynamic LVOT gradient. The LV is underfilled and there is no pulmonary congestion. Which initial physiological strategy is most appropriate?',
 'Give cautious volume replacement with hemodynamic reassessment',[('Begin a dobutamine infusion', 'Inotropic support can be considered for selected pump-failure mechanisms, but increasing basal contractility can intensify the supplied dynamic obstruction. EF alone does not define the shock treatment.'), ('Begin an intravenous nitrate infusion', 'Afterload reduction can worsen dynamic LVOTO and hypotension. A strategy useful in another HF setting cannot be transferred without evaluating this mechanism.'), ('Begin intravenous loop diuresis', 'Diuresis treats congestion when present. Further preload reduction in this small, noncongested obstructive presentation can intensify systolic anterior motion and impair forward flow.')],
 [S('Define the Shock Mechanism at the Bedside','**Takotsubo shock can reflect pump failure, RV involvement, dynamic obstruction or important mitral regurgitation.** These mechanisms require different and sometimes opposing treatment strategies.',
    'Assess the LVOT with Doppler and examine basal contraction, filling and systolic anterior motion. A low global EF does not establish that additional inotropy will improve output.',
    'Catecholamine-driven contractility and vasodilation can aggravate an obstructive phenotype. Continue to assess coronary and other competing causes when the clinical course is not explained.'),
  S('Treat Demonstrated Obstruction Without Ignoring Perfusion','**Cautious volume replacement is appropriate for a genuine deficit when congestion is absent.** Reassess lungs, gradient and perfusion rather than giving a fixed large volume to every hypotensive patient.',
    'Beta blockade can reduce dynamic contractility in selected patients, but severe hypotension, bradycardia or acute decompensated failure can make it unsafe. Do not turn the mechanism into an unmonitored instruction to give a beta blocker during shock.',
    'Escalate expert hemodynamic and temporary-support assessment if perfusion remains inadequate. Support selection must consider obstruction and both ventricular functions; a device that changes afterload or filling can alter the gradient. Monitor QT and rhythm, and assess severe apical akinesis for thrombus or an anticoagulation indication. Reversible wall-motion abnormalities do not make acute shock benign. Repeat echo as the syndrome evolves because the dominant mechanism can change, and taper supportive therapy according to recovery rather than the original EF alone.')],['TAK2','TAK2024'])

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Early Cancer-Therapy-Related Cardiac Dysfunction',
 'During anthracycline surveillance, an asymptomatic patient’s LVEF changes from 62% to 55% and GLS from −20% to −16%, using comparable high-quality studies. How is this classified by the ESC 2022 asymptomatic CTRCD framework?',
 'Mild asymptomatic cancer-therapy-related cardiac dysfunction',[
 ('No CTRCD because EF remains above fifty percent','The mild category can be identified with preserved EF and a significant relative GLS decline or a new biomarker rise. Preserved EF alone does not exclude early dysfunction.'),
 ('Moderate asymptomatic CTRCD','Moderate dysfunction requires a new EF in the forty-to-forty-nine-percent range with the appropriate accompanying change or evidence. The supplied EF is fifty-five percent.'),
 ('Severe asymptomatic CTRCD','Severe asymptomatic dysfunction requires a new EF below forty percent. A twenty-percent relative strain change is not the same as an EF of twenty percent.')],
 [S('Calculate Relative Strain Change Correctly','**GLS is assessed by the reduction in absolute strain magnitude relative to baseline.** Here, (20 − 16)/20 × 100 equals a 20% relative decline.',
    'That exceeds the greater-than-fifteen-percent threshold. Do not use the four-percentage-point difference as though it were the relative percentage change, and do not interpret a less negative value as improved shortening.',
    'Compare image quality, loading conditions, rhythm and measurement method before assigning a change. A baseline study makes an early abnormality more interpretable than an isolated value.'),
  S('Classify Severity and Make a Treatment-Specific Plan','**With EF at least 50% and a new relative GLS decline greater than 15%, this meets mild asymptomatic CTRCD.** The category describes detected dysfunction, not an automatic instruction to permanently stop every cancer treatment.',
    'Coordinate oncology and cardiology review, assess biomarkers and symptoms and consider cardioprotective therapy and closer surveillance according to the particular treatment and risk. Anthracycline and HER2-targeted therapy pathways are not identical.',
    'A high cumulative exposure, prior cardiovascular disease or new symptoms can change the response. More severe dysfunction may require interruption and HF treatment, but a single generic rule for all agents can compromise either cardiac or cancer care. Explain the finding, verify the trend and document the reassessment schedule. Cardiovascular risk-factor control remains relevant, while a normal troponin would not erase the supplied strain criterion. Distinguish an early surveillance signal from symptomatic HF, and avoid waiting for advanced EF decline before reviewing the regimen.')],['ONCOESC2022'],
 table=(['Asymptomatic ESC 2022 severity','Definition'],[
 ['Mild','EF ≥50% plus new relative GLS decline >15% and/or new biomarker elevation'],
 ['Moderate','EF 40–49% with ≥10-point fall; or <10-point fall with GLS decline >15% and/or biomarker elevation'],
 ['Severe','New EF <40%']]))

add(SECTIONS[5][0],'Alcohol and Tachycardia as Reversible Contributors',
 'A patient with newly recognized DCM has sustained rapid AF over several months and substantial alcohol exposure. Coronary evaluation is reassuring and CMR shows no definite scar. Which plan best addresses the possible contributors?',
 'Treat AF, alcohol exposure and HF together, then reassess recovery and susceptibility',[('Prioritize AF control and defer alcohol intervention until ventricular reassessment', 'Arrhythmia control may allow recovery in predominantly arrhythmia-mediated dysfunction. Here substantial alcohol exposure is a concurrent actionable contributor, so waiting to address it leaves another plausible myocardial stressor in place.'), ('Prioritize abstinence and defer an active rhythm or rate strategy until ventricular reassessment', 'Abstinence can improve alcohol-associated dysfunction, but sustained rapid AF can independently impair ventricular function. Both contributors should be treated alongside HF care rather than testing alcohol withdrawal while tachycardia persists.'), ('Treat both exposures but omit familial assessment because CMR shows no scar', 'Absence of detected scar can support recovery potential but does not exclude genetic susceptibility. A pedigree and phenotype-guided familial assessment remain relevant when acquired exposures coexist with DCM.')],
 [S('Treat Plausible Contributors in Parallel','**Arrhythmia-mediated dysfunction and alcohol-associated myocardial injury can coexist.** Neither diagnosis should be used to ignore the other while an untreated stressor continues.',
    'Control the clinically important tachyarrhythmia through an appropriate rate or rhythm strategy, and provide a feasible abstinence and support plan. Start indicated HF therapy with renal, pressure and electrolyte monitoring.',
    'Review thyroid disease, other toxins, medications, nutritional factors and the pedigree as appropriate. A nondiagnostic CMR does not make the etiological assessment complete.'),
  S('Use Recovery as Evidence Without Overinterpreting It','**Improvement after treatment supports a reversible component, but does not prove that the myocardium had no underlying susceptibility.** Pathogenic TTN truncating variants can contribute to vulnerability in exposure-associated disease.',
    'Plan repeat ventricular assessment after adequate arrhythmia control and treatment, and document the achieved rhythm/rate rather than assuming the intervention was effective. Persistent dysfunction warrants renewed review of adherence, cause and treatment adequacy.',
    'Do not automatically stop HF therapy because EF normalizes; recovery can represent remission with relapse risk. Embolic prevention for AF follows the patient’s appropriate clinical framework, not the assumption that a reversible trigger removes every thromboembolic concern. Genetic counseling and testing are selected according to the full phenotype and potential implications for care and relatives. New syncope or sustained ventricular arrhythmia requires prompt risk assessment rather than waiting for a routine recovery scan. The practical objective is to remove actionable stressors while continuing to investigate and monitor the myocardial disease.')],['CMP2023','TTNALCOHOL'])

def highlight_values(text):
    """Apply the requested yellow emphasis to numerical teaching values only."""
    pattern=r'(?<![\w.])(?:[<>≤≥]\s*)?\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:[–−-]\d+(?:\.\d+)?)?\s*(?:mg/m²|mg/kg/day|mg/kg|mg|mmHg|mm|ms|%)(?!\w)'
    return re.sub(pattern,lambda m:'=='+m.group(0)+'==',text)

